(function(){(window.PhysicaNeetYear=window.PhysicaNeetYear||{})[2016]=[{id:"neet-2016-q001",pn:1,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Effect of catalyst",q:"The addition of a catalyst during a chemical reaction alters which of the following quantities ?",o:["Internal energy","Enthalpy","Activation energy","Entropy"],c:2,s:`A catalyst gives the reaction a new, shorter path through an intermediate.
This path has a lower energy barrier, so the activation energy decreases.
Internal energy, enthalpy and entropy depend only on the initial and final states, which stay the same.
So only the activation energy is altered.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Kinetics"},{id:"neet-2016-q002",pn:2,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure",st:"VSEPR repulsion order",q:"Predict the correct order among the following:",o:["lone pair-lone pair > bond pair-bond pair > lone pair \u2013 bond pair","bond pair \u2013 bond pair > lone pair \u2013 bond pair > lone pair \u2013 lone pair","lone pair \u2013 bond pair > bond pair \u2013 bond pair > lone pair \u2013 lone pair","lone pair \u2013 lone pair > lone pair \u2013 bond pair > bond pair \u2013 bond pair"],c:3,s:`In VSEPR theory a lone pair is held by only one nucleus, so it spreads out and occupies more space.
More space means more repulsion.
The order is lone pair-lone pair > lone pair-bond pair > bond pair-bond pair.
This matches option (4).`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure"},{id:"neet-2016-q003",pn:3,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Amines",st:"Basicity of amines",q:"The correct statement regarding the basicity of arylamines is:",o:["Arylamines are generally more basis than alkylamines because the nitrogen lone-pair electrons are not delocalized by interaction with the aromatic ring \u03C0 electron system.","Arylamines are generally more basic than alkylamines because of aryl group.","Arylamines are generally more basic than alkylamines, because the nitrogen atom in arylamines is sp-hybridized","Arylamines are generally less basic than alkylamines because the nitrogen lone-pair electrons are delocalized by interaction with the aromatic ring \u03C0 electron system."],c:3,s:`In an arylamine the lone pair on N is delocalised into the benzene ring by resonance.
In an alkylamine the lone pair stays localised on N and is easily donated.
A less available lone pair means weaker basicity.
So arylamines are less basic than alkylamines, as option (4) says.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Amines"},{id:"neet-2016-q004",pn:4,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Nitric acid reactions",q:"When copper is heated with conc. HNO\u2083 it produces:",o:["Cu(NO\u2083)\u2082 and NO","Cu(NO\u2083)\u2082, NO and NO\u2082","Cu(NO\u2083)\u2082 and N\u2082O","Cu(NO\u2083)\u2082 and NO\u2082"],c:3,s:`Concentrated HNO\u2083 is reduced to NO\u2082 (a reddish-brown gas) by copper.
Cu + 4HNO\u2083 \u2192 Cu(NO\u2083)\u2082 + 2H\u2082O + 2NO\u2082
The products are Cu(NO\u2083)\u2082 and NO\u2082.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2016-q005",pn:5,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Haloalkanes and Haloarenes",st:"Elimination, substitution, addition",q:`For the following reactions:
(a) CH\u2083CH\u2082CH\u2082Br + KOH \u2192 CH\u2083CH = CH\u2082 + KBr + H\u2082O
(b) 2-bromopropane (CH\u2083)\u2082CHBr + KOH \u2192 propan-2-ol (CH\u2083)\u2082CHOH + KBr
(c) cyclohexene + Br\u2082 \u2192 1,2-dibromocyclohexane
Which of the following statements is correct ?`,o:["(a) is elimination, (b) is substitution and (c) addition reaction.","(a) is elimination, (b) and (c) are substitution reactions.","(a) is substitution, (b) and (c) are addition reactions.","(a) and (b) are elimination reactions and (c) is addition reaction."],c:0,s:`(a) HBr is removed and a C=C double bond forms, so it is elimination.
(b) Br is replaced by OH, so it is substitution.
(c) Br\u2082 adds across the C=C double bond of cyclohexene, so it is addition.
This matches option (1).`,fig:{w:340,h:195,alt:"Reactions: (a) 1-bromopropane with KOH gives propene; (b) 2-bromopropane with KOH gives propan-2-ol; (c) cyclohexene with bromine gives 1,2-dibromocyclohexane",d:[["t",4,14,"(a) CH\u2083CH\u2082CH\u2082Br + KOH \u2192 CH\u2083CH = CH\u2082 + KBr + H\u2082O"],["t",4,66,"(b)"],["l",70,76,56,60],["l",70,76,84,60],["l",70,76,70,94],["t",50,56,"H\u2083C","e"],["t",88,56,"CH\u2083"],["t",70,108,"Br","m"],["t",104,84,"+ KOH \u2192"],["l",210,76,196,60],["l",210,76,224,60],["l",210,76,210,94],["t",190,56,"H\u2083C","e"],["t",228,56,"CH\u2083"],["t",210,108,"OH","m"],["t",262,84,"+ KBr"],["t",4,159,"(c)"],["p","M45 138L59.7 146.5L59.7 163.5L45 172L30.3 163.5L30.3 146.5Z"],["l",57.1,150,57.1,160],["t",68,159,"+ Br\u2082 \u2192"],["p","M160 138L174.7 146.5L174.7 163.5L160 172L145.3 163.5L145.3 146.5Z"],["l",175,146.5,185,138],["t",187,136,"Br"],["l",175,163.5,185,172],["t",187,182,"Br"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Haloalkanes and Haloarenes"},{id:"neet-2016-q006",pn:6,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Structure of Atom",st:"Quantum numbers",q:"Two electrons occupying the same orbital are distinguished by:",o:["Magnetic quantum number","Azimuthal quantum number","Spin quantum number","Principal quantum number"],c:2,s:`Electrons in the same orbital have the same n, l and m\u2097.
By the Pauli exclusion principle they must differ in the fourth quantum number, the spin quantum number (+\xBD and -\xBD).`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Structure of Atom"},{id:"neet-2016-q007",pn:7,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers",st:"Williamson synthesis",q:"The reaction (cyclopentanol + NaH \u2192 sodium cyclopentoxide, then Me\u2013I \u2192 methoxycyclopentane) can be classified as:",o:["Alcohol formation reaction","Dehydration reaction","Williamson alcohol synthesis reaction","Williamson ether synthesis reaction"],c:3,s:`NaH removes the acidic H of cyclopentanol and gives sodium cyclopentoxide (R\u2013ONa).
The alkoxide then reacts with methyl iodide: R\u2013X + R\u2032\u2013ONa \u2192 R\u2013O\u2013R\u2032 + NaX.
An ether (methoxycyclopentane) forms, so this is Williamson ether synthesis.`,fig:{w:450,h:80,alt:"Cyclopentanol reacts with NaH to give sodium cyclopentoxide, which reacts with Me\u2013I to give methoxycyclopentane",d:[["p","M66 48L54.9 63.2L37.1 57.4L37.1 38.6L54.9 32.8Z"],["l",66,48,80,48],["t",81,53,"OH"],["l",108,48,150,48,"a"],["t",129,40,"NaH","ms"],["p","M212 48L200.9 63.2L183.1 57.4L183.1 38.6L200.9 32.8Z"],["l",212,48,222,48],["t",222,53,"O"],["t",234,44,"\u2296","s"],["t",248,53,"Na"],["t",266,44,"\u2295","s"],["l",290,48,332,48,"a"],["t",311,40,"Me\u2013I","ms"],["p","M382 48L370.9 63.2L353.1 57.4L353.1 38.6L370.9 32.8Z"],["l",382,48,392,48],["t",392,53,"O"],["l",402,44,410,34],["t",411,32,"Me"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers"},{id:"neet-2016-q008",pn:8,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Electronic configuration of lanthanoids",q:"The electronic configurations of Eu (Atomic No. 63), Gd(Atomic No. 64) and Tb (Atomic No. 65) are:",o:["[Xe]4f\u20765d\xB96s\xB2, [Xe]4f\u20775d\xB96s\xB2 and [Xe]4f\u20796s\xB2","[Xe]4f\u20765d\xB96s\xB2, [Xe]4f\u20775d\xB96s\xB2 and [Xe]4f\u20785d\xB96s\xB2","[Xe]4f\u20776s\xB2, [Xe]4f\u20775d\xB96s\xB2 and [Xe]4f\u20796s\xB2","[Xe]4f\u20776s\xB2, [Xe]4f\u20786s\xB2 and [Xe]4f\u20785d\xB96s\xB2"],c:2,s:`Eu (63): [Xe]4f\u20776s\xB2, a half-filled 4f subshell, which is extra stable.
Gd (64): [Xe]4f\u20775d\xB96s\xB2, the extra electron goes to 5d to keep 4f half-filled.
Tb (65): [Xe]4f\u20796s\xB2.
This matches option (3).`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The d- and f-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2016-q009",pn:9,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Solutions",st:"Elevation of boiling point",q:"At 100\xBAC the vapour pressure of a solution of 6.5 g of a solute in 100 g water is 732 mm. If K_b = 0.52, the boiling point of this solution will be:",o:["100\xBAC","102\xBAC","103\xBAC","101\xBAC"],c:3,s:`Vapour pressure of pure water at 100\xBAC is 760 mm.
(P\xB0 - P)/P = m \xD7 M_A/1000, with M_A = 18.
(760 - 732)/732 = m \xD7 18/1000, so m = 2.125 mol/kg.
\u0394T_b = K_b \xD7 m = 0.52 \xD7 2.125 = 1.10 \xBAC.
Boiling point = 100 + 1.10 = 101.1 \xBAC, about 101\xBAC.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Solutions"},{id:"neet-2016-q010",pn:10,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Hydrocarbons",st:"Conformations of ethane",q:"The correct statement regarding the comparison of staggered and eclipsed conformations of ethane, is:",o:["The eclipsed conformation of ethane is more stable than staggered conformation, because eclipsed conformation has no torsional strain.","The eclipsed conformation of ethane is more stable than staggered conformation, even though the eclipsed conformation has torsional strain.","The staggered conformation of ethane is more stable than eclipsed conformation, because staggered conformation has no torsional strain.","The staggered conformation of ethane is less stable the an eclipsed conformation, because staggered conformation has torsional strain."],c:2,s:`In the staggered form the C\u2013H bonds of the two carbons are as far apart as possible, so there is no torsional strain.
In the eclipsed form the C\u2013H bonds are in line and repel, giving torsional strain.
So staggered is more stable than eclipsed.`,sfig:{w:300,h:232,alt:"Ethane with all hydrogens shown, then staggered and eclipsed Newman projections of ethane",d:[["t",60,14,"H","m"],["t",80,14,"H","m"],["l",60,18,60,28],["l",80,18,80,28],["t",32,46,"H"],["l",43,42,53,42],["t",60,46,"C","m"],["l",67,42,73,42],["t",80,46,"C","m"],["l",87,42,97,42],["t",99,46,"H"],["l",60,50,60,60],["l",80,50,80,60],["t",60,72,"H","m"],["t",80,72,"H","m"],["t",150,46,"(ethane)"],["c",70,140,16],["l",70,140,40.6,123],["t",33.6,123,"H","m"],["l",70,140,99.4,123],["t",106.4,123,"H","m"],["l",70,140,70,174],["t",70,186,"H","m"],["l",70,124,70,106],["t",70,102,"H","m"],["l",56.1,148,40.6,157],["t",33.6,165,"H","m"],["l",83.9,148,99.4,157],["t",106.4,165,"H","m"],["t",150,144,"V/s","m"],["c",235,140,16],["l",235,140,235,106],["t",235,102,"H","m"],["l",235,140,205.6,157],["t",198.6,165,"H","m"],["l",235,140,264.4,157],["t",271.4,165,"H","m"],["l",232.8,124.2,230.3,106.3],["t",229.2,102.4,"H","m"],["l",222.4,149.9,208.2,160.9],["t",201.9,169.9,"H","m"],["l",249.8,146,266.5,152.7],["t",273.9,159.7,"H","m"],["t",40,200,"staggered"],["t",40,215,"conformer"],["t",200,200,"Eclipsed"],["t",200,215,"conformer"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrocarbons"},{id:"neet-2016-q011",pn:11,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Surface Chemistry",st:"Thermodynamics of adsorption",q:"Which one of the following characteristics is associated with adsorption ?",o:["\u0394G, \u0394H and \u0394S all are negative","\u0394G and \u0394H are negative but \u0394S is positive","\u0394G and \u0394S are negative but \u0394H is positive","\u0394G is negative but \u0394H and \u0394S are positive"],c:0,s:`Adsorption is spontaneous, so \u0394G is negative.
Molecules get held on the surface and lose freedom, so \u0394S is negative.
From \u0394G = \u0394H - T\u0394S with \u0394S negative, \u0394H must be negative for \u0394G to be negative.
So \u0394G, \u0394H and \u0394S are all negative.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Surface Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2016-q012",pn:12,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure",st:"Shapes of xenon compounds",q:`Match the compounds given in column I with the hybridisation and shape given in column II and mark the correct option.
Column I: (a) XeF\u2086  (b) XeO\u2083  (c) XeOF\u2084  (d) XeF\u2084
Column II: (i) distorted octahedral  (ii) square planar  (iii) pyramidal  (iv) square pyramidal`,o:["(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],c:3,s:`XeF\u2086: sp\xB3d\xB3, 6 bond pairs + 1 lone pair, distorted octahedral.
XeO\u2083: sp\xB3, 3 bond pairs + 1 lone pair, pyramidal.
XeOF\u2084: sp\xB3d\xB2, 5 bond pairs + 1 lone pair, square pyramidal.
XeF\u2084: sp\xB3d\xB2, 4 bond pairs + 2 lone pairs, square planar.
So (a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii), which is option (4).`,sfig:{w:200,h:452,alt:"Structures of XeF\u2086 (distorted octahedral), XeO\u2083 (pyramidal), XeOF\u2084 (square pyramidal) and XeF\u2084 (square planar) with lone pairs",d:[["r",60,30,80,40],["l",60,30,140,70],["l",60,70,140,30],["t",56,29,"F","e"],["t",144,29,"F"],["t",56,80,"F","e"],["t",144,80,"F"],["l",100,22,100,78],["t",100,14,"F","m"],["t",100,94,"F","m"],["p","M100 50C112 52 120 40 114 33C110 30 104 34 100 50"],["c",108,24,1.3,"F"],["c",116,24,1.3,"F"],["t",100,108,"XeF\u2086","ms"],["t",100,150,"Xe","m"],["c",96,134,1.3,"F"],["c",104,134,1.3,"F"],["l",95,155,80,168],["l",97,154,82,167],["l",105,155,120,168],["l",103,154,118,167],["l",99,157,99,172],["l",102,157,102,172],["t",76,181,"O","m"],["t",124,181,"O","m"],["t",100,186,"O","m"],["t",100,202,"XeO\u2083","ms"],["c",96,224,1.3,"F"],["c",104,224,1.3,"F"],["r",60,240,80,40],["l",60,240,89,256],["l",111,264,140,280],["l",60,280,89,264],["l",111,256,140,240],["t",56,239,"F","e"],["t",144,239,"F"],["t",56,290,"F","e"],["t",144,290,"F"],["t",100,264,"Xe","ms"],["l",98,280,98,298],["l",102,280,102,298],["t",100,308,"O","m"],["t",100,322,"XeOF\u2084","ms"],["c",96,350,1.3,"F"],["c",104,350,1.3,"F"],["r",60,366,80,40],["l",60,366,89,382],["l",111,390,140,406],["l",60,406,89,390],["l",111,382,140,366],["t",56,365,"F","e"],["t",144,365,"F"],["t",56,416,"F","e"],["t",144,416,"F"],["t",100,390,"Xe","ms"],["c",96,424,1.3,"F"],["c",104,424,1.3,"F"],["t",100,442,"XeF\u2084","ms"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure"},{id:"neet-2016-q013",pn:13,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids",st:"Keto-enol tautomerism",q:"The correct statement regarding a carbonyl compound with a hydrogen atom on its alpha-carbon, is:",o:["a carbonyl compound with a hydrogen atom on its alpha-carbon rapidly equilibrates with its corresponding enol and this process is known as aldehyde-ketone equilibration.","a carbonyl compound with a hydrogen atom on its alpha-carbon rapidly equilibrates with its corresponding enol and this process is known as carbonylation","a carbonyl compound with a hydrogen atom on its alpha-carbon rapidly equilibrates with its corresponding enol and this process is known as keto-enol tautomerism.","a carbonyl compound with a hydrogen atom on its alpha-carbon never equilibrates with its corresponding enol."],c:2,s:`A carbonyl compound with an \u03B1-hydrogen shifts that H to oxygen and forms an enol (C=C\u2013OH).
The two forms are in quick equilibrium.
This is called keto-enol tautomerism.`,sfig:{w:320,h:100,alt:"Keto form H\u2013CH\u2082\u2013C(=O)\u2013R in equilibrium with enol form CH\u2082=C(OH)\u2013R, with curved arrows showing the hydrogen shift",d:[["c",30,30,8],["t",30,34,"H","ms"],["l",38,30,50,30],["t",50,34,"CH\u2082"],["l",74,30,88,30],["t",96,34,"C","m"],["l",103,30,116,30],["t",118,34,"R"],["l",94,38,94,52],["l",98,38,98,52],["t",96,64,"O","m"],["p","M30 20Q62 2 92 22","a"],["p","M24 38Q22 74 84 64","a"],["p","M106 56Q120 62 108 72","a"],["l",150,27,190,27,"a"],["l",190,35,150,35,"a"],["t",208,34,"CH\u2082"],["l",236,30,252,30],["l",236,34,252,34],["t",258,34,"C","m"],["l",265,30,278,30],["t",280,34,"R"],["l",258,38,258,52],["t",258,64,"O","m"],["l",266,60,276,60],["c",286,60,8],["t",286,64,"H","ms"],["p","M236 22Q220 4 208 22","a"],["p","M250 62Q226 62 214 40","a"],["t",96,90,"(Keto)","m"],["t",268,90,"(Enol)","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids"},{id:"neet-2016-q014",pn:14,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Peptide bond",q:"In a protein molecule various amino acids are linked together by:",o:["\u03B2-glycosidic bond","peptide bond","dative bond","\u03B1-glycosidic bond"],c:1,s:`The \u2013COOH of one amino acid reacts with the \u2013NH\u2082 of the next and loses water.
The \u2013CO\u2013NH\u2013 amide link formed is the peptide bond.
This joins amino acids into proteins.`,sfig:{w:480,h:132,alt:"Two amino acids join with loss of water to form a dipeptide; the CO\u2013NH link is marked as the peptide bond",d:[["t",22.4,48.5,"H\u2082N","ms"],["t",49.6,48.5,"C","ms"],["t",72,48.5,"C","ms"],["t",93.6,48.5,"O","ms"],["t",114.4,48.5,"H","ms"],["t",127.2,48.5,"+","ms"],["t",140.8,48.5,"H","ms"],["t",160,48.5,"N","ms"],["t",182.4,48.5,"C","ms"],["t",204,48.5,"C","ms"],["t",230.4,48.5,"OH","ms"],["l",34.4,45,44.8,45],["l",54.4,45,67.2,45],["l",76.8,45,87.2,45],["l",100,45,109.6,45],["l",145.6,45,155.2,45],["l",164.8,45,176.8,45],["l",187.2,45,199.2,45],["l",208.8,45,221.6,45],["t",49.6,17.5,"R","ms"],["l",49.6,22,49.6,37],["t",49.6,79.5,"H","ms"],["l",49.6,53,49.6,67],["l",70.8,53,70.8,67],["l",74.8,53,74.8,67],["t",72.8,79.5,"O","ms"],["t",160,17.5,"H","ms"],["l",160,22,160,37],["t",182.4,17.5,"H","ms"],["l",182.4,22,182.4,37],["t",204,17.5,"O","ms"],["l",202,22,202,37],["l",206,22,206,37],["t",182.4,79.5,"R","ms"],["l",182.4,53,182.4,67],["r",84.8,30,65.6,24,"d"],["l",244.8,45,298.4,45,"a"],["t",272,63.5,"\u2013H\u2082O","ms"],["t",312.8,48.5,"NH\u2082","ms"],["t",341.6,48.5,"C","ms"],["t",362.4,48.5,"C","ms"],["t",384,48.5,"N","ms"],["t",405.6,48.5,"C","ms"],["t",433.6,48.5,"C","ms"],["t",460.8,48.5,"OH","ms"],["l",324.8,45,336.8,45],["l",346.4,45,357.6,45],["l",367.2,45,378.4,45],["l",388.8,45,400,45],["l",410.4,45,428,45],["l",438.4,45,452.8,45],["t",341.6,17.5,"R","ms"],["l",341.6,22,341.6,37],["t",341.6,79.5,"H","ms"],["l",341.6,53,341.6,67],["l",361.2,53,361.2,67],["l",365.2,53,365.2,67],["t",363.2,79.5,"O","ms"],["t",384,17.5,"H","ms"],["l",384,22,384,37],["t",405.6,17.5,"H","ms"],["l",405.6,22,405.6,37],["t",433.6,17.5,"O","ms"],["l",431.6,22,431.6,37],["l",435.6,22,435.6,37],["t",405.6,79.5,"R","ms"],["l",405.6,53,405.6,67],["l",374.4,104,374.4,52,"a"],["t",374.4,123.5,"Peptide bond","ms"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2016-q015",pn:15,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Metallurgy processes",q:`Match items of Column I with the items of Column II and assign the correct code:
Column I: (a) Cyanide process  (b) Froth floatation process  (c) Electrolytic reduction  (d) Zone refining
Column II: (i) Ultrapure Ge  (ii) Dressing of ZnS  (iii) Extraction of Al  (iv) Extraction of Au  (v) Purification of Ni`,o:["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(v)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(v), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],c:3,s:`Cyanide process: gold is dissolved as Na[Au(CN)\u2082] and then displaced by Zn, so it is used for extraction of Au.
4Au + 8NaCN + O\u2082 + 2H\u2082O \u2192 4Na[Au(CN)\u2082] + 4NaOH
2Na[Au(CN)\u2082] + Zn \u2192 2Au + Na\u2082[Zn(CN)\u2084]
Froth floatation: sulphide ores such as ZnS are concentrated (dressing).
Electrolytic reduction (Hall-H\xE9roult, with cryolite): extraction of Al.
Zone refining: semiconductors like Ge and Si are made ultrapure.
So (a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i).
Note: Chapter placed in the d- and f-Block list because no metallurgy chapter exists in the NEET chapter list.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The d- and f-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2016-q016",pn:16,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Chemistry in Everyday Life",st:"Analgesics",q:"Which of the following is an analgesic ?",o:["Penicillin","Streptomycin","Chloromycetin","Novalgin"],c:3,s:`Novalgin is a pain-killer (analgesic).
Penicillin, streptomycin and chloromycetin are antibiotics.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemistry in Everyday Life"},{id:"neet-2016-q017",pn:17,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Oxoacids of phosphorus",q:"Which is the correct statement for the given acids ? (phosphinic acid H\u2083PO\u2082 and phosphonic acid H\u2083PO\u2083)",o:["Phosphinic acid is a monoprotic acid while phosphonic acid is a diprotic acid","Phosphinic acid is a diprotic acid while phosphonic acid is a monoprotic acid","Both are triprotic acids","Both are diprotic acids"],c:0,s:`Only P\u2013OH hydrogens are acidic; P\u2013H hydrogens are not.
Phosphinic acid H\u2083PO\u2082 has one P\u2013OH, so it is monoprotic.
Phosphonic acid H\u2083PO\u2083 has two P\u2013OH, so it is diprotic.`,fig:{w:220,h:250,alt:"Structures of H\u2083PO\u2082 (P with =O, one OH and two H) and H\u2083PO\u2083 (P with =O, two OH and one H)",d:[["t",4,70,"H\u2083PO\u2082","s"],["l",117.5,26,117.5,46],["l",122.5,26,122.5,46],["t",120,20,"O","m"],["t",120,64,"P","m"],["t",76,70,"HO","e"],["l",80,64,111,54],["l",129,54,154,64],["t",156,70,"H"],["l",120,68,120,86],["t",120,100,"H","m"],["t",4,195,"H\u2083PO\u2083","s"],["l",117.5,151,117.5,171],["l",122.5,151,122.5,171],["t",120,145,"O","m"],["t",120,189,"P","m"],["t",76,195,"HO","e"],["l",80,189,111,179],["l",129,179,154,189],["t",156,195,"H"],["l",120,193,120,211],["t",120,225,"OH","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2016-q018",pn:18,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques",st:"Hybridisation in carbanion",q:"The pair of electron in the given carbanion, CH\u2083C\u2261C\u207B, is present in which of the following orbitals ?",o:["sp\xB3","sp\xB2","sp","2p"],c:2,s:`Both carbons of the triple bond in propyne are sp hybridised.
The negative charge sits on the terminal sp carbon, so its lone pair is in an sp orbital.`,sfig:{w:150,h:66,alt:"Propyne carbanion CH\u2083\u2013C\u2261C\u207B: sp\xB3 carbon joined by a single bond to an sp carbon, which is joined to the terminal sp carbon carrying the negative charge by one \u03C3 and two \u03C0 bonds",d:[["t",6,34,"CH\u2083"],["t",20,52,"sp\xB3","ms"],["l",38,30,58,30],["t",66,34,"C","m"],["t",66,52,"sp","ms"],["l",74,26,104,26],["l",74,30,104,30],["l",74,34,104,34],["t",89,18,"\u03C3","ms"],["t",91,52,"2\u03C0","ms"],["t",112,34,"C"],["t",121,27,"\u2212","s"],["t",124,52,"sp","ms"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques"},{id:"neet-2016-q019",pn:19,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure",st:"Bond angles in CH4, NH3, H2O",q:"Consider the molecules CH\u2084, NH\u2083 and H\u2082O. Which of the given statements is false?",o:["The H \u2013 O \u2013 H bond angle in H\u2082O is larger than the H \u2013 C \u2013 H bond angle in CH\u2084","The H \u2013 O \u2013 H bond angle in H\u2082O is smaller than the H \u2013 N \u2013 H bond angle in NH\u2083","The H \u2013 C \u2013 H bond angle in CH\u2084 is larger than the H \u2013 C \u2013 H bond angle in NH\u2083","The H \u2013 C \u2013 H bond angle in CH\u2084, the H \u2013 N \u2013 H bond angle in NH\u2083, and the H \u2013 O \u2013 H bond angle in H\u2082O are all greater than 90\xBA"],c:0,s:`All three are sp\xB3 hybridised, but the lone pairs on N and O repel more than bond pairs.
CH\u2084 has 109\xB028\u2032, NH\u2083 has 107\xB0 and H\u2082O has 105\xB0.
So H\u2013O\u2013H (105\xB0) is smaller than H\u2013C\u2013H (109\xB028\u2032).
Statement (1) is false.
Note: Statement (3) is worded 'H\u2013C\u2013H in NH\u2083' in the paper; it is a printing slip for H\u2013N\u2013H and the key (1) is unaffected.`,sfig:{w:330,h:160,alt:"Table: CH\u2084 > NH\u2083 > H\u2082O in bond angle; all sp\xB3; shapes tetrahedral, pyramidal, bent/V; angles 109\xB028\u2032, 107\xB0, 105\xB0; repulsion bp\u2013bp, lp\u2013bp, lp\u2013lp",d:[["t",4,18,"Bond angle","s"],["t",100,18,"CH\u2084","m"],["t",148,18,">","m"],["t",196,18,"NH\u2083","m"],["t",244,18,">","m"],["t",290,18,"H\u2082O","m"],["c",193,5,1.2,"F"],["c",199,5,1.2,"F"],["c",287,5,1.2,"F"],["c",293,5,1.2,"F"],["c",287,24,1.2,"F"],["c",293,24,1.2,"F"],["t",4,60,"Shape","s"],["t",4,84,"Bond angle","s"],["t",4,108,"Repulsion","s"],["t",100,44,"sp\xB3","ms"],["t",196,44,"sp\xB3","ms"],["t",290,44,"sp\xB3","ms"],["t",100,60,"tetrahedral","ms"],["t",196,60,"pyramidal","ms"],["t",290,60,"bent/V","ms"],["t",100,84,"109\xB028\u2032","ms"],["t",196,84,"107\xB0","ms"],["t",290,84,"105\xB0","ms"],["t",100,108,"bp\u2013bp","ms"],["t",196,108,"lp\u2013bp","ms"],["t",290,108,"lp\u2013lp","ms"],["l",100,124,300,124,"a"],["t",100,142,"Repulsion \u2191  Bond angle \u2193","s"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure"},{id:"neet-2016-q020",pn:20,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Reactions of SO2",q:"Which one of the following statements is correct when SO\u2082 is passed through acidified K\u2082Cr\u2082O\u2087 solution ?",o:["The solution is decolourized","SO\u2082 is reduced","Green Cr\u2082(SO\u2084)\u2083 is formed","The solution turns blue"],c:2,s:`Acidified K\u2082Cr\u2082O\u2087 is an oxidising agent and oxidises SO\u2082 to sulphate.
Orange Cr\u2082O\u2087\xB2\u207B (Cr\u2076\u207A) is reduced to green Cr\xB3\u207A.
K\u2082Cr\u2082O\u2087 + 3SO\u2082 + H\u2082SO\u2084 \u2192 K\u2082SO\u2084 + Cr\u2082(SO\u2084)\u2083 + H\u2082O
SO\u2082 is oxidised, not reduced, and green Cr\u2082(SO\u2084)\u2083 forms.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2016-q021",pn:21,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)",st:"Spontaneity conditions",q:"The correct thermodynamic conditions for the spontaneous reaction at all temperatures is :",o:["\u0394H > 0 and \u0394S < 0","\u0394H < 0 and \u0394S > 0","\u0394H < 0 and \u0394S < 0","\u0394H < 0 and \u0394S = 0"],c:1,s:`\u0394G = \u0394H - T\u0394S, and a process is spontaneous when \u0394G < 0.
If \u0394H is negative and \u0394S is positive, then -T\u0394S is negative too.
So \u0394G is negative at every temperature.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2016-q022",pn:22,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Polymers",st:"Natural rubber",q:"Natural rubber has :",o:["All trans-configuration","Alternate cis - and trans-configuration","Random cis- and trans-configuration","All cis-configuration"],c:3,s:`Natural rubber is cis-1,4-polyisoprene.
All the double bonds in the chain have the cis configuration.`,sfig:{w:385,h:92,alt:"Natural rubber chain segment with CH\u2083 and H on the same side of every C=C (cis, cis, cis)",d:[["t",6.4,39.5,"...CH\u2082","s"],["t",22.4,24.3,"CH\u2083","s"],["l",44,35.2,57.6,28.8],["l",44,22.4,57.6,27.2],["t",57.6,32.3,"C = C","s"],["l",92,25.6,101.6,20.8],["t",102.4,22.7,"H","s"],["l",89.6,33.6,101.6,37.6],["t",102.4,42.7,"CH\u2082","s"],["l",124,36.8,135.2,31.2],["t",136,32.3,"CH\u2082","s"],["l",158.4,31.2,171.2,37.6],["t",172,42.7,"C = C","s"],["l",173.6,44,162.4,49.6],["t",140.8,55.5,"CH\u2083","s"],["l",202.4,36,215.2,31.2],["t",216,32.3,"CH\u2082","s"],["l",202.4,42.4,215.2,46.4],["t",216,52.3,"H","s"],["l",238.4,31.2,250.4,37.6],["t",252,42.7,"CH\u2082","s"],["l",273.6,36.8,284,31.2],["t",249.6,23.5,"CH\u2083","s"],["l",271.2,22.4,284,28],["t",284,32.3,"C = C","s"],["l",313.6,25.6,328,20.8],["t",330.4,22.7,"H","s"],["l",313.6,32,328,37.6],["t",330.4,42.7,"CH\u2082 ....","s"],["t",63.2,70.7,"cis","ms"],["t",176,69.1,"cis","ms"],["t",299.2,69.1,"cis","ms"],["t",202.4,88.3,"Natural Rubber","ms"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Polymers"},{id:"neet-2016-q023",pn:23,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties",st:"Periodic trends",q:"In Which of the following options the order of arrangement does not agree with the variation of property indicate against it ?",o:["B < C < N < O (increasing first ionisation enthalpy)","I < Br < Cl < F (increasing electron gain enthalpy)","Li < Na < K < Rb (increasing metallic radius)","Al\xB3\u207A < Mg\xB2\u207A < Na\u207A < F\u207B (increasing ionic size)"],a:"1, 2",s:`Option (1): N has a half-filled 2p\xB3, so its ionisation enthalpy is higher than O; the real order is B < C < O < N, so (1) does not agree.
Option (2): F has a small 2p orbital, so electron repulsion is high and its electron gain enthalpy is less than Cl; the real order is I < Br < F < Cl, so (2) does not agree.
Options (3) and (4) are correct.
Note: Printed key is '1, 2' (two options); both (1) and (2) do not agree with the property order.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties"},{id:"neet-2016-q024",pn:24,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers",st:"Cis-trans diols",q:"Which of the following reagents would distinguish cis-cyclopenta-1, 2-diol from the trans-isomer ?",o:["Ozone","MnO\u2082","Aluminium isopropoxide","Acetone"],c:3,s:`The two OH groups of the cis diol are on the same side, so with acetone (acid catalyst, -H\u2082O) they form a cyclic acetal (ketal).
In the trans diol the OH groups are on opposite sides of the ring and cannot reach the same carbon, so there is no reaction.
So acetone distinguishes them.`,sfig:{w:300,h:208,alt:"cis-Cyclopentane-1,2-diol with acetone and acid gives a cyclic ketal (loss of water); the trans diol with acetone gives no reaction",d:[["p","M32 9.6L53.3 25.1L45.2 50.1L18.8 50.1L10.7 25.1Z"],["l",19.2,50.4,13.6,60.8],["l",44.8,50.4,50.4,60.8],["t",6.4,69.1,"O","s"],["c",23.2,65.6,4.8],["t",23.2,69.1,"H","ms"],["t",44,69.1,"O","s"],["c",61.6,65.6,4.8],["t",61.6,69.1,"H","ms"],["t",22.4,91.5,"(cis)","s"],["t",72,44.3,"+ CH\u2083 \u2013 C \u2013 CH\u2083","s"],["l",120,46.4,120,56.8],["l",123.2,46.4,123.2,56.8],["c",121.6,65.6,4.8],["t",121.6,69.1,"O","ms"],["l",170.4,40.8,221.6,40.8,"a"],["t",185.6,33.9,"H\u2295","ms"],["t",192,55.5,"\u2013H\u2082O","ms"],["p","M253.6 8L274.9 23.5L266.8 48.5L240.4 48.5L232.3 23.5Z"],["l",240.8,48.8,236,61.6],["l",266.4,48.8,271.2,59.2],["t",236,69.9,"O","ms"],["t",271.2,67.5,"O","ms"],["l",238.4,71.2,250.4,78.4],["l",268.8,68.8,259.2,77.6],["t",254.4,85.9,"C","ms"],["l",252,86.4,247.2,97.6],["l",256.8,86.4,264.8,97.6],["t",246.4,107.5,"Me","ms"],["t",270.4,107.5,"Me","ms"],["p","M32 117.6L53.3 133.1L45.2 158.1L18.8 158.1L10.7 133.1Z"],["l",19.2,158.4,14.4,165.6],["t",14.4,173.9,"OH","ms"],["l",44.8,158.4,39.2,144],["t",32.8,141.1,"HO","ms"],["t",72,149.9,"+ CH\u2083 \u2013 C \u2013 CH\u2083","s"],["l",120,152,120,162.4],["l",123.2,152,123.2,162.4],["t",121.6,173.9,"O","ms"],["l",170.4,146.4,221.6,146.4,"a"],["t",192,139.5,"H\u2295","ms"],["t",260,148.3,"No reaction","ms"],["t",38.4,197.1,"(trans)","ms"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers"},{id:"neet-2016-q025",pn:25,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Calcium carbide with nitrogen",q:"The product obtained as a result of a reaction of nitrogen with CaC\u2082 is",o:["CaCN","CaCN\u2083","Ca\u2082CN","Ca(CN)\u2082"],a:"Bonus",s:`Nitrogen reacts with calcium carbide on heating to give calcium cyanamide.
CaC\u2082 + N\u2082 \u2192 CaCN\u2082 + C
The product CaCN\u2082 is not among the options.
Note: Printed key is Bonus: none of the options is CaCN\u2082.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The s-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2016-q026",pn:26,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Surface Chemistry",st:"Types of colloids",q:"Fog is a colloidal solution of :",o:["Gas in liquid","Solid in gas","Gas in gas","Liquid in gas"],c:3,s:`Fog has tiny liquid droplets (dispersed phase) spread in air (dispersion medium).
So it is a colloid of liquid in gas (an aerosol).`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Surface Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2016-q027",pn:27,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Halogen bond enthalpy",q:"Which one of the following orders in correct for the bond dissociation enthalpy of halogen molecules ?",o:["Cl\u2082 > Br\u2082 > F\u2082 > I\u2082","Br\u2082 > I\u2082 > F\u2082 > Cl\u2082","F\u2082 > Cl\u2082 > Br\u2082 > I\u2082","I\u2082 > Br\u2082 > Cl\u2082 > F\u2082"],c:0,s:`Going down the group the bond becomes longer and weaker, so Cl\u2082 > Br\u2082 > I\u2082.
F\u2082 is an exception: its very small atoms put the lone pairs close together and the strong lone pair repulsion weakens the F\u2013F bond.
So F\u2082 falls below Cl\u2082 and Br\u2082 but stays above I\u2082.
Order: Cl\u2082 > Br\u2082 > F\u2082 > I\u2082.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2016-q028",pn:28,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"States of Matter",st:"Graham's law of effusion",q:"Equal moles of hydrogen and oxygen gases are placed in a container with a pin-hole through which both can escape. What fraction of the oxygen escapes in the time required for one-half of the hydrogen to escape ?",o:["1/4","3/8","1/2","1/8"],c:3,s:`By Graham's law, rate of effusion \u221D 1/\u221AM.
r(O\u2082)/r(H\u2082) = \u221A(M_H\u2082/M_O\u2082) = \u221A(2/32) = \u221A(1/16) = 1/4.
In the same time, moles of O\u2082 escaped : moles of H\u2082 escaped = 1 : 4.
Half of the H\u2082 escapes, so O\u2082 escaped = (1/4) \xD7 (1/2) = 1/8 of the starting amount (the moles are equal).`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"States of Matter"},{id:"neet-2016-q029",pn:29,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"The Solid State",st:"Density of a unit cell",q:"Lithium has a bcc structure. Its density is 530 kg m\u207B\xB3 and its atomic mass is 6.94 g mol\u207B\xB9. Calculate the edge length of a unit cell of Lithium metal. (N_A = 6.02 \xD7 10\xB2\xB3 mol\u207B\xB9)",o:["352 pm","527 pm","264 pm","154 pm"],c:0,s:`For bcc, Z = 2 atoms per unit cell.
d = Z\xB7M/(N_A\xB7a\xB3), so a\xB3 = Z\xB7M/(N_A\xB7d).
M = 6.94 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 kg mol\u207B\xB9.
a\xB3 = (2 \xD7 6.94 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)/(6.02 \xD7 10\xB2\xB3 \xD7 530) = 4.35 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2079 m\xB3 = 43.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2070 m\xB3.
a = 3.52 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 m = 352 pm.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The Solid State"},{id:"neet-2016-q030",pn:30,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Solutions",st:"Raoult's law vapour composition",q:"Which of the following statements about the composition of the vapour over an ideal 1 : 1 molar mixture of benzene and toluene is correct ? Assume that the temperature is constant at 25\xBAC. (Given, Vapour Pressure Data at 25\xBAC, benzene = 12.8 kPa, toluene = 3.85 kPa)",o:["The vapour will contain a higher percentage of toluene","The vapour will contain equal amounts of benzene and toluene","Not enough information is given to make a prediction","The vapour will contain a higher percentage of benzene"],c:3,s:`Mole fraction in vapour: y_A = p_A/P = (P\xB0_A\xB7x_A)/P.
The liquid is 1 : 1, so x_A = x_B and y is proportional to the vapour pressure of the pure liquid.
Benzene (12.8 kPa) has a higher vapour pressure than toluene (3.85 kPa).
So the vapour has a higher percentage of benzene.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Solutions"},{id:"neet-2016-q031",pn:31,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Coordination Compounds",st:"Metal carbonyl bonding",q:"Which of the following has longest C \u2013 O bond length ? (Free C \u2013 O bond length in CO is 1.128 \xC5)",o:["[Co(CO)\u2084]\u207B","[Fe(CO)\u2084]\xB2\u207B","[Mn(CO)\u2086]\u207A","Ni(CO)\u2084"],c:1,s:`In metal carbonyls the metal donates electrons back into the \u03C0* orbital of CO (back bonding).
More back bonding means a lower C\u2013O bond order and a longer C\u2013O bond.
Oxidation states: Ni(CO)\u2084 Ni\u2070, [Mn(CO)\u2086]\u207A Mn\u207A, [Co(CO)\u2084]\u207B Co\u207B\xB9, [Fe(CO)\u2084]\xB2\u207B Fe\u207B\xB2.
Fe\u207B\xB2 has the most negative charge, so the most electron density to give back to CO.
So [Fe(CO)\u2084]\xB2\u207B has the longest C\u2013O bond.
Note: Options (1) and (2) are printed without their negative charges; the charges are written as in the solution, for example [Co(CO)\u2084]\u207B.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Coordination Compounds"},{id:"neet-2016-q032",pn:32,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Acidity of oxoacids of chlorine",q:"Among the following the correct order of acidity is :",o:["HClO < HClO\u2082 < HClO\u2083 < HClO\u2084","HClO\u2084 < HClO < HClO\u2083 < HClO\u2084","HClO\u2084 < HClO\u2082 < HClO < HClO\u2083","HClO\u2083 < HClO\u2084 < HClO\u2082 < HClO"],c:0,s:`Oxidation state of Cl: HClO +1, HClO\u2082 +3, HClO\u2083 +5, HClO\u2084 +7.
More oxygen atoms give more resonance stabilisation of the anion and pull electrons from O\u2013H more strongly.
So acidity rises with oxidation state: HClO < HClO\u2082 < HClO\u2083 < HClO\u2084.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2016-q033",pn:33,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Hydrocarbons",st:"Alkylation of alkynes",q:"In the correct reaction sequence: HC\u2261CH \u2192 (1) NaNH\u2082/liq. NH\u2083, (2) CH\u2083CH\u2082Br \u2192 X; X \u2192 (1) NaNH\u2082/liq. NH\u2083, (2) CH\u2083CH\u2082Br \u2192 Y. X and Y are :",o:["X = 2-Butyne ; Y = 3-Hexyne","X = 2-Butyne ; Y = 2-Hexyne","X = 1-Butyne ; Y = 2-Hexyne","X = 1-Butyne ; Y = 3-Hexyne"],c:3,s:`NaNH\u2082 removes the acidic \u2261C\u2013H and gives HC\u2261C\u207BNa\u207A.
This reacts with CH\u2083CH\u2082Br: HC\u2261C\u2013CH\u2082CH\u2083 (1-butyne) = X.
X has a terminal \u2261C\u2013H again; NaNH\u2082 then gives CH\u2083CH\u2082C\u2261C\u207BNa\u207A.
It reacts with CH\u2083CH\u2082Br: CH\u2083CH\u2082C\u2261CCH\u2082CH\u2083 (3-hexyne) = Y.
Note: The question stem is printed in a damaged form; it is reconstructed from the reaction scheme in the printed solution (HC\u2261CH as the start).`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrocarbons"},{id:"neet-2016-q034",pn:34,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Solubility product",q:"MY and NY\u2083, two nearly insoluble salts, have the same K_SP values of 6.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 at room temperature. Which statement would be true in regard to MY and NY\u2083 ?",o:["The molar solubility of MY in water is less than that of NY\u2083","The salts MY and NY\u2083 are more soluble in 0.5 M KY than in pure water","The addition of the salt of KY to solution of MY and NY\u2083 will have no effect on their solubilities","The molar solubilities of MY and NY\u2083 in water are identical"],c:0,s:`For MY: K_sp = S\xB2, so S = \u221A(6.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3) = \u221A(62 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2074) \u2248 8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 M.
For NY\u2083 \u21CC N\xB3\u207A + 3Y\u207B: K_sp = S \xD7 (3S)\xB3 = 27S\u2074.
S = (6.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3/27)^(1/4) = (0.2296 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3)^(1/4) \u2248 3.89 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 M.
So MY is less soluble than NY\u2083, option (1).
KY adds common ion Y\u207B, which lowers solubility, so (2) and (3) are wrong.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Equilibrium"},{id:"neet-2016-q035",pn:35,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Hydrocarbons",st:"Nitration of benzene",q:"Consider the nitration of benzene using mixed conc. H\u2082SO\u2084 and HNO\u2083. If a large amount of KHSO\u2084 is added to the mixture, the rate of nitration will be :",o:["slower","uncharged","doubled","faster"],c:0,s:`The attacking species NO\u2082\u207A forms by: H\u2082SO\u2084 + HNO\u2083 \u21CC NO\u2082\u207A + HSO\u2084\u207B + H\u2082O.
KHSO\u2084 gives K\u207A and HSO\u2084\u207B, which adds HSO\u2084\u207B (common ion).
The equilibrium shifts backward, so less NO\u2082\u207A forms.
So nitration becomes slower.
Note: Option (2) 'uncharged' is printed so (probably meaning 'unchanged').`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrocarbons"},{id:"neet-2016-q036",pn:36,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids",st:"Reaction with primary amines",q:"The product formed by the reaction of an aldehyde with a primary amine is :",o:["Ketone","Carboxylic acid","Aromatic acid","Schiff base"],c:3,s:`An aldehyde adds a primary amine (R'\u2013NH\u2082) and then loses water (nucleophilic addition-elimination).
The product R\u2013CH=N\u2013R' (an imine) is called a Schiff base.`,sfig:{w:350,h:112,alt:"An aldehyde R\u2013CHO reacts with a primary amine H\u2082N\u2013R to give the general Schiff base structure R\u2013CH=N\u2013R\u2032",d:[["t",16.4,21.8,"R","ms"],["l",23.9,22.1,43.9,33.5],["t",14.4,56.9,"H","ms"],["l",23.9,48.6,43.9,37.3],["t",44.9,38.8,"C = O","s"],["t",89.5,38.8,"+","s"],["t",111.3,19.9,"H","ms"],["l",119.9,21.1,137.9,30.6],["t",110.4,55.9,"H","ms"],["l",118,48.6,137,39.1],["t",138.9,38.8,"N \u2013 R","s"],["l",182.6,34.4,230.1,34.4,"a"],["t",241.5,19.9,"R","ms"],["l",249.1,20.1,268.1,29.6],["t",239.6,55.9,"H","ms"],["l",247.2,47.7,267.1,38.2],["t",270.9,37,"C = N","s"],["l",308,39.1,324.1,48.6],["t",333.6,59.8,"R\u2032","ms"],["t",63.8,78.8,"Aldehyde","ms"],["t",154.1,78.8,"Primary","ms"],["t",154.1,96.8,"amines","ms"],["t",289.9,77.8,"General structure","ms"],["t",289.9,95.8,"or Schiff base","ms"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids"},{id:"neet-2016-q037",pn:37,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Nernst equation for hydrogen electrode",q:"The pressure of H\u2082 required to make the potential of H\u2082-electrode zero in pure water at 298 K is :",o:["10\u207B\xB9\xB2 atm","10\u207B\xB9\u2070 atm","10\u207B\u2074 atm","10\u207B\xB9\u2074 atm"],c:3,s:`Electrode reaction: 2H\u207A + 2e\u207B \u2192 H\u2082(g), E\xB0 = 0.
E = 0 - (0.0591/2) log(P_H\u2082/[H\u207A]\xB2).
For E = 0, log(P_H\u2082/[H\u207A]\xB2) = 0, so P_H\u2082 = [H\u207A]\xB2.
In pure water [H\u207A] = 10\u207B\u2077 M.
P_H\u2082 = (10\u207B\u2077)\xB2 = 10\u207B\xB9\u2074 atm.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Electrochemistry"},{id:"neet-2016-q038",pn:38,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Nucleic acid sugars",q:"The correct statement regarding RNA and DNA respectively is :",o:["The sugar component in RNA is ribose and the sugar component in DNA is 2-dexyribose","The sugar component in RNA is arabinose and the sugar component in DNA is ribose","The sugar component in RNA is 2'-dexyribose and the sugar component in DNA is arabinose","The sugar component in RNA is arabinose and the sugar component in DNA is 2'-deoxyribose"],c:0,s:`RNA contains the pentose sugar D-ribose.
DNA contains 2'-deoxyribose, in which the OH at C-2' is replaced by H.
So option (1).`,sfig:{w:210,h:380,alt:"Furanose rings of D-ribose (OH at C2) and 2-deoxyribose (H at C2), carbons numbered 1 to 5",d:[["p","M65.5 62.5L100 47.5"],["p","M124 47.5L164.5 64L146.5 103L82 103L65.5 62.5"],["t",100,42,"HO\u2013CH\u2082","es"],["t",65.5,19.5,"5","ms"],["t",53.5,70.5,"4","ms"],["t",74.5,114,"3","ms"],["t",142,112.5,"2","ms"],["t",172,70.5,"1","ms"],["l",67,59.5,77.5,47.5],["t",112,49.5,"O","ms"],["l",164.5,61,169,49],["t",172,39,"OH","ms"],["t",88,81,"H","ms"],["t",139,81,"H","ms"],["t",64,99,"H","ms"],["t",163,99,"H","ms"],["l",82,106,88,115],["t",92.5,127.5,"OH","ms"],["l",146.5,106,149.5,115],["t",149.5,127.5,"OH","ms"],["p","M79 280L113.5 265"],["p","M137.5 265L178 281.5L160 320.5L95.5 320.5L79 280"],["t",113.5,259.5,"HO\u2013CH\u2082","es"],["t",79,237,"5","ms"],["t",67,288,"4","ms"],["t",88,331.5,"3","ms"],["t",155.5,330,"2","ms"],["t",185.5,288,"1","ms"],["l",80.5,277,91,265],["t",125.5,267,"O","ms"],["l",178,278.5,182.5,266.5],["t",185.5,256.5,"OH","ms"],["t",101.5,298.5,"H","ms"],["t",152.5,298.5,"H","ms"],["t",77.5,316.5,"H","ms"],["t",176.5,316.5,"H","ms"],["l",95.5,323.5,101.5,332.5],["t",106,345,"OH","ms"],["l",160,323.5,163,332.5],["t",163,345,"H","ms"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2016-q039",pn:39,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Reducing and non-reducing sugars",q:"Which one given below is a non-reducing sugar ?",o:["Lactose","Glucose","Sucrose","Maltose"],c:2,s:`A sugar is reducing only if it has a free aldehyde or hemiacetal (free anomeric) carbon.
In sucrose the C-1 of \u03B1-D-glucose and C-2 of \u03B2-D-fructose are joined in the glycosidic bond, so neither has a free reducing group.
Lactose, glucose and maltose are reducing.`,sfig:{w:350,h:162,alt:"Sucrose: \u03B1-D-glucose pyranose ring joined through an oxygen (glycosidic linkage) from C1 to C2 of the \u03B2-D-fructose furanose ring",d:[["p","M56 62L94 54"],["p","M110 54L126 74L104 102L46 102L32 76L56 62"],["t",102,57,"O","ms"],["l",56,62,62,44],["t",74,34,"CH\u2082OH","ms"],["t",74,20,"6","ms"],["l",56,62,46,52],["t",40,48,"H","ms"],["t",68,74,"5","ms"],["l",32,76,26,66],["t",21,62,"H","ms"],["l",32,76,22,88],["t",13,98,"OH","ms"],["t",44,83,"4","ms"],["l",46,102,38,112],["t",28,119,"HO","ms"],["l",46,102,50,114],["t",52,126,"H","ms"],["t",58,94,"3","ms"],["l",104,102,110,114],["t",112,126,"OH","ms"],["l",104,102,101,92],["t",99,86,"H","ms"],["t",86,95,"2","ms"],["l",126,74,130,62],["t",133,56,"H","ms"],["t",114,83,"1","ms"],["l",126,74,140,74],["r",140,63,46,22,"d"],["t",163,78,"O","ms"],["l",186,74,200,74],["t",163,104,"glycosidic","ms"],["t",163,120,"linkage","ms"],["p","M200 74L232 60"],["p","M250 60L270 78L262 104L214 104L200 74"],["t",241,63,"O","ms"],["l",200,74,204,56],["t",206,46,"HO\u2013CH\u2082","ms"],["t",206,28,"1","ms"],["t",210,86,"2","ms"],["l",214,104,216,116],["t",218,128,"OH","ms"],["l",214,104,221,94],["t",224,88,"H","ms"],["t",234,98,"3","ms"],["l",262,104,262,116],["t",262,128,"H","ms"],["t",274,100,"4","ms"],["l",262,104,254,94],["t",252,90,"HO","ms"],["l",270,78,278,64],["t",283,58,"H","ms"],["t",284,80,"5","ms"],["l",270,78,284,96],["t",306,108,"CH\u2082OH","ms"],["t",292,122,"6","ms"],["t",80,152,"\u03B1-D-glucose","ms"],["t",245,152,"\u03B2-D-fructose","ms"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2016-q040",pn:40,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Hydrogen",st:"Properties of hydrogen",q:"Which of the following statement about hydrogen is incorrect ?",o:["Hydrogen never acts as cation in ionic salts","Hydronium ion, H\u2083O\u207A exists freely in solution","Dihydrogen does not act as a reducing agent","Hydrogen has three isotopes of which tritium is the most common"],a:"3,4",s:`(1) is correct: in ionic salts hydrogen exists as the hydride ion H\u207B.
(2) is correct: H\u207A is hydrated and exists as H\u2083O\u207A in solution.
(3) is incorrect: H\u2082 reduces some metal ions and metal oxides, e.g. H\u2082 + Pd\xB2\u207A \u2192 Pd + 2H\u207A.
(4) is incorrect: the most common isotope is protium, not tritium.
Note: Printed key is '3,4' (two options); both (3) and (4) are incorrect statements.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrogen"},{id:"neet-2016-q041",pn:41,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Clausius-Clapeyron equation",q:"Consider the following liquid-vapour equilibrium: Liquid \u21CC Vapour. Which of the following relations is correct ?",o:["d ln P/dT = -\u0394H_v/RT","d ln P/dT\xB2 = -\u0394H_v/T\xB2","d ln P/dT = \u0394H_v/RT\xB2","d ln G/dT\xB2 = \u0394H_v/RT\xB2"],c:2,s:`Clausius-Clapeyron: P = A\xB7e^(-\u0394H_v/RT).
Take ln: ln P = ln A - \u0394H_v/(RT).
Differentiate with respect to T: d ln P/dT = 0 + (\u0394H_v/R)\xB7d(-T\u207B\xB9)... that is, d/dT(-1/T) = +1/T\xB2.
So d ln P/dT = \u0394H_v/(RT\xB2), option (3).`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Equilibrium"},{id:"neet-2016-q042",pn:42,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques",st:"Optical activity in biphenyls",q:"Which of the following biphenyls is optically active ?",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:0,s:`Biphenyls with large groups at the ortho positions cannot rotate freely about the central bond.
The two rings are then held in different planes, and with different groups on each ring there is no plane of symmetry.
In (1) the two rings each carry Br and I in ortho positions, so the molecule is chiral (optically active).
In (3) the ring with two identical CH\u2083 groups and (2), (4) with few or meta groups have a plane of symmetry or free rotation.`,fig:{w:410,h:196,alt:"Four biphenyls: (1) Br and I on both ortho positions next to the central bond, (2) one ortho I on each ring, (3) two ortho CH\u2083 on one ring and a plain second ring, (4) NO\u2082 meta on one ring and I meta on the other",d:[["t",4,50,"(1)"],["p","M82 46L72 63.3L52 63.3L42 46L52 28.7L72 28.7Z"],["l",76.8,48.1,71.2,57.8],["l",52.8,57.8,47.2,48.1],["l",56.4,32.1,67.6,32.1],["p","M136 46L126 63.3L106 63.3L96 46L106 28.7L126 28.7Z"],["l",130.8,48.1,125.2,57.8],["l",106.8,57.8,101.2,48.1],["l",110.4,32.1,121.6,32.1],["l",82,46,96,46],["l",72,28.7,78,18.3],["t",86.5,7.800000000000001,"Br","es"],["l",72,63.3,78,73.7],["t",86.5,88.2,"I","es"],["l",106,28.7,100,18.3],["t",91.5,7.800000000000001,"Br","s"],["l",106,63.3,100,73.7],["t",91.5,88.2,"I","s"],["t",214,50,"(2)"],["p","M292 46L282 63.3L262 63.3L252 46L262 28.7L282 28.7Z"],["l",286.8,48.1,281.2,57.8],["l",262.8,57.8,257.2,48.1],["l",266.4,32.1,277.6,32.1],["p","M346 46L336 63.3L316 63.3L306 46L316 28.7L336 28.7Z"],["l",340.8,48.1,335.2,57.8],["l",316.8,57.8,311.2,48.1],["l",320.4,32.1,331.6,32.1],["l",292,46,306,46],["l",282,28.7,288,18.3],["t",296.5,7.800000000000001,"I","es"],["l",316,63.3,310,73.7],["t",301.5,88.2,"I","s"],["t",4,146,"(3)"],["p","M82 142L72 159.3L52 159.3L42 142L52 124.7L72 124.7Z"],["l",76.8,144.1,71.2,153.8],["l",52.8,153.8,47.2,144.1],["l",56.4,128.1,67.6,128.1],["p","M136 142L126 159.3L106 159.3L96 142L106 124.7L126 124.7Z"],["l",130.8,144.1,125.2,153.8],["l",106.8,153.8,101.2,144.1],["l",110.4,128.1,121.6,128.1],["l",82,142,96,142],["l",72,124.7,78,114.3],["t",86.5,103.8,"CH\u2083","es"],["l",72,159.3,78,169.7],["t",86.5,184.2,"CH\u2083","es"],["t",214,146,"(4)"],["p","M292 142L282 159.3L262 159.3L252 142L262 124.7L282 124.7Z"],["l",286.8,144.1,281.2,153.8],["l",262.8,153.8,257.2,144.1],["l",266.4,128.1,277.6,128.1],["p","M346 142L336 159.3L316 159.3L306 142L316 124.7L336 124.7Z"],["l",340.8,144.1,335.2,153.8],["l",316.8,153.8,311.2,144.1],["l",320.4,128.1,331.6,128.1],["l",292,142,306,142],["l",262,124.7,256,114.3],["t",250.5,103.8,"O\u2082N","ms"],["l",336,159.3,342,169.7],["t",347.5,184.2,"I","ms"]]},sfig:{w:140,h:120,alt:"Biphenyl of option (1) with Br Br above and I I below the central bond",d:[["p","M64 60L52 80.8L28 80.8L16 60L28 39.2L52 39.2Z"],["l",57.8,62.5,51,74.1],["l",29,74.1,22.2,62.5],["l",33.3,43.4,46.7,43.4],["p","M128.8 60L116.8 80.8L92.8 80.8L80.8 60L92.8 39.2L116.8 39.2Z"],["l",122.6,62.5,115.8,74.1],["l",93.8,74.1,87,62.5],["l",98.1,43.4,111.5,43.4],["l",64,60,80.8,60],["l",52,39.2,59.2,26.7],["t",68.8,14.3,"Br","es"],["l",52,80.8,59.2,93.3],["t",68.8,109.7,"I","es"],["l",92.8,39.2,85.6,26.7],["t",76,14.3,"Br","s"],["l",92.8,80.8,85.6,93.3],["t",76,109.7,"I","s"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques"},{id:"neet-2016-q043",pn:43,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Biological role of Ca and Mg",q:"Which of the following statements is false ?",o:["Ca\xB2\u207A ions are important blood in clotting","Ca\xB2\u207A ions are not important in maintaining the regular beating of heart","Mg\xB2\u207A ions are important in the green parts of plants","Mg\xB2\u207A ions form a complex with ATP"],c:1,s:`Ca\xB2\u207A is needed for blood clotting, nerve impulse and the regular beating of the heart.
Mg\xB2\u207A is the central atom of chlorophyll and forms a complex with ATP.
So the false statement is (2): Ca\xB2\u207A is important for the heartbeat.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The s-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2016-q044",pn:44,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"The Solid State",st:"Radius ratio and coordination number",q:"The ionic radii of A\u207A and B\u207B ions are 0.98 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 m and 1.81 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 m. The coordination number of each ion in AB is :",o:["4","8","2","6"],c:3,s:`Radius ratio = r(A\u207A)/r(B\u207B) = 0.98/1.81 = 0.541.
The range 0.414 to 0.732 corresponds to an octahedral hole.
So the coordination number is 6.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The Solid State"},{id:"neet-2016-q045",pn:45,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"First order half-life",q:"The rate of a first order reaction is 0.04 mol l\u207B\xB9 s\u207B\xB9 at 10 seconds and 0.03 mol l\u207B\xB9 s\u207B\xB9 at 20 seconds after initiation of the reaction. The half-life period of the reaction is :",o:["34.1 s","44.1 s","54.1 s","24.1 s"],c:3,s:`For a first order reaction rate = k[A], so rate \u221D concentration.
The ratio of rates equals the ratio of concentrations over the 10 s gap (from 10 s to 20 s).
k = (2.303/10) log(0.04/0.03) = 0.2303 \xD7 log(4/3) = 0.2303 \xD7 0.1249 = 0.0285 s\u207B\xB9.
t\xBD = 0.693/k = 0.693/0.0285 = 24.1 s.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Kinetics"},{id:"neet-2016-q046",pn:46,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Insulin structure",q:"The two polypeptides of human insulin are linked together by",o:["Phosphodiester bond","Covalent bond","Disulphide bridges","Hydrogen bonds"],c:2,s:`Human insulin has two chains, A (21 amino acids) and B (30 amino acids).
They are joined by disulphide bridges (\u2013S\u2013S\u2013).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Chemical Coordination and Integration"},{id:"neet-2016-q047",pn:47,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Endosperm types",q:"The coconut water from tender coconut represents :",o:["Fleshy mesocarp","Free nuclear proembryo","Free nuclear endosperm","Endocarp"],c:2,s:`Coconut water is the free-nuclear endosperm: the endosperm nucleus divides many times without wall formation, leaving a liquid with many free nuclei.
The white kernel is the later cellular endosperm.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2016-q048",pn:48,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Plasmid features",q:"Which of the following is not a feature of the plasmids ?",o:["Circular structure","Transferable","Single-stranded","Independent replication"],c:2,s:`Plasmids are small, circular, double-stranded DNA molecules.
They replicate independently of the chromosome and can be transferred between cells.
So single-stranded is not a feature.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2016-q049",pn:49,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"National aquatic animal",q:"Which is the National Aquatic Animal of India ?",o:["River dolphin","Blue whale","Sea-horse","Gangetic shark"],c:0,s:"The Ganges river dolphin (Platanista gangetica) is the National Aquatic Animal of India.",sub:"Zoology",ch:"Biodiversity and Conservation"},{id:"neet-2016-q050",pn:50,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Plant Growth and Development",st:"Bioassay of auxin",q:"The Avena curvature is used for bioassay of",o:["GA\u2083","IAA","Ethylene","ABA"],c:1,s:`The Avena (oat) coleoptile curvature test measures auxin.
A block of agar with auxin placed on one side of a decapitated coleoptile makes it bend, and the angle of bending is proportional to the amount of IAA.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Growth and Development"},{id:"neet-2016-q051",pn:51,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Causes of extinction",q:"Which of the following is the most important cause of animals and plants being driven to extinction ?",o:["Alien species invasion","Habitat loss and fragmentation","Co-extinctions","Over-exploitation"],c:1,s:`Among the four causes of biodiversity loss (the 'evil quartet'), habitat loss and fragmentation is the most important.
It is the most common cause of species extinction across the globe.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biodiversity and Conservation"},{id:"neet-2016-q052",pn:52,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Reproductive Health",st:"Contraceptive methods",q:`Which of the following approaches does not give the defined action of contraceptive ?
Approach - Action:`,o:["Intra uterine devices - Increase phagocytosis of sperms, suppress sperm motility and fertilizing capacity of sperm","Hormonal contraceptives - Prevent/retard entry of sperm, prevent ovulation and fertilization","Vasectomy - Prevents spermatogenesis","Barrier methods - Prevent fertilization"],c:2,s:`IUDs increase phagocytosis of sperm and reduce their motility and fertilising power: correct.
Pills and other hormonal methods stop ovulation and make the cervix hostile to sperm: correct.
Barrier methods physically stop sperm meeting the ovum: correct.
Vasectomy cuts the vas deferens so sperm cannot reach the semen; spermatogenesis in the testis continues.
So (3) does not match.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Reproductive Health"},{id:"neet-2016-q053",pn:53,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Linkage",q:"In a testcross involving F1 dihybrid flies, more parental-type offspring were produced than the recombinant-type offspring. This indicates -",o:["Chromosomes failed to separate during meiosis","The two genes are linked and present on the same chromosome","Both of the characters are controlled by more than one gene","The two genes are located on two different chromosomes"],c:1,s:`For genes on different chromosomes, a testcross gives parental and recombinant types in equal numbers (1:1:1:1).
More parental than recombinant types means the genes are linked on the same chromosome.
They stay together most of the time, and only crossing over makes a few recombinants.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2016-q054",pn:54,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Structure of fats",q:"A typical fat molecule is made up of -",o:["One glycerol and three fatty acid molecules","One glycerol and one fatty acid molecule","Three glycerol and three fatty acid molecules","Three glycerol molecules and one fatty acid molecule"],c:0,s:`A typical fat is a triglyceride.
One glycerol (three \u2013OH groups) is esterified with three fatty acid molecules.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biomolecules (Biology)"},{id:"neet-2016-q055",pn:55,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Inheritance terms",q:`Match the terms in Column-I with their description in Column-II and choose the correct option :
Column-I: (a) Dominance (b) Codominance (c) Pleiotropy (d) Polygenic inheritance
Column-II: (i) Many genes govern a single character (ii) In a heterozygous organism only one allele expresses itself (iii) In a heterozygous organism both alleles express themselves fully (iv) A single gene influences many characters`,o:["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],c:0,s:`Dominance: only one allele expresses in the heterozygote, so (a)-(ii).
Codominance: both alleles express fully, so (b)-(iii).
Pleiotropy: one gene affects many characters, so (c)-(iv).
Polygenic inheritance: many genes control one character, so (d)-(i).`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2016-q056",pn:56,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Pollination and pollen-pistil interaction",q:"Which of the following statements is not correct ?",o:["Insects that consume pollen or nectar without bringing about pollination are called pollen / nector robbers.","Pollen germination and pollen tube growth are regulated by chemical components of pollen interacting with those of the pistil","Some reptiles have also been reported as pollinators in some plant species","Pollen grains of many species can germinate on the stigma of a flower, but only one pollen tube of the same species grows into the style"],c:3,s:`(1), (2) and (3) are correct statements.
In (4), many pollen tubes of the same species can grow into the style, so the 'only one' claim is wrong.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2016-q057",pn:57,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Periplaneta americana features",q:"Which of the following features is not present in Periplaneta americana ?",o:["Indeterminate and radial cleavage during embryonic development","Exoskeleton composed of N-acetylglucosamine","Metamerically segmented body","Schizocoelom as body cavity"],c:0,s:`Periplaneta (an arthropod) has a chitin exoskeleton (polymer of N-acetylglucosamine), a metamerically segmented body and a schizocoelom (haemocoel).
Its embryo shows spiral and determinate cleavage.
So indeterminate, radial cleavage is not present.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Structural Organisation in Animals"},{id:"neet-2016-q058",pn:58,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Plant pigments",q:"Water soluble pigments found in plant cell vacuoles are -",o:["Chlorophylls","Carotenoids","Anthocyanine","Xanthophylls"],c:2,s:`Anthocyanins are water-soluble pigments in the cell sap of vacuoles.
Chlorophylls, carotenoids and xanthophylls are fat-soluble and sit in plastids.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2016-q059",pn:59,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Polyploidy",q:"A cell at telophase stage is observed by a student in a plant brought from the field. He tells his teacher that this cell is not like other cells at telophase stage. There is no formation of cell plate and thus the cell is containing more number of chromosomes as compared to other dividing cells. This would results in -",o:["Polyploidy","Somaclonal variation","Polyteny","Aneuploidy"],c:0,s:`If the cell plate does not form, cytokinesis fails after the chromosomes have separated.
The cell then holds the full set of chromosomes of both daughter nuclei, a whole extra set.
A gain of whole sets is polyploidy (aneuploidy would be a gain or loss of single chromosomes).`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2016-q060",pn:60,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants",st:"C4 pathway",q:"A plant in your garden avoids photorespiratory losses, has improved water use efficiency, shows high rates of photosynthesis at high temperatures and has improved efficiency of nitrogen utilization. In which of the following physiological groups would you assign this plant ?",o:["C4","CAM","Nitrogen fixer","C3"],c:0,s:`C4 plants have Kranz anatomy, which keeps CO\u2082 high around RuBisCO and avoids photorespiration.
They use water efficiently, photosynthesise well at high temperature and use nitrogen efficiently.
So the plant is C4.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants"},{id:"neet-2016-q061",pn:61,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Autoimmunity",q:"In higher vertebrates, the immune system can distinguish self-cells and non-self. If this property is lost due to genetic abnormally and it attacks self-cells, then it leads to",o:["Graft rejection","Auto-immune disease","Active immunity","Allergic response"],c:1,s:"When the immune system fails to tell self from non-self and attacks body cells, the result is an auto-immune disease (for example rheumatoid arthritis).",sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2016-q062",pn:62,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants",st:"Emerson effect",q:"Emerson's enhancement effect and Red drop have been instrumental in the discovery of",o:["Two photosystems operating simultaneously","Photophosphorylation and cyclic electron transport","Oxidative phosphorylation","Photophosphorylation and non-cyclic electron transport"],c:0,s:`Red drop: quantum yield falls sharply in far-red light (beyond about 680 nm).
Emerson's enhancement: red plus far-red light together give a rate greater than the sum of each alone.
This showed two photosystems working together in the light reaction.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants"},{id:"neet-2016-q063",pn:63,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Gymnosperms",q:"Select the correct statement",o:["Salvinia, Ginkgo and Pinus all are gymnosperms","Sequoia is one of the tallest trees","The leaves of gymnosperms are not well adapted to extremes of climate","Gymnosperms are both homosporous and heterosporous"],c:1,s:`Salvinia is a pteridophyte, so (1) is wrong.
Gymnosperm leaves are well adapted to extreme climate, so (3) is wrong.
Gymnosperms are all heterosporous, so (4) is wrong.
Sequoia sempervirens (red-wood) is among the tallest trees, so (2) is correct.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2016-q064",pn:64,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Mitosis vs meiosis",q:"Which of the following is not a characteristic feature during mitosis in somatic cells ?",o:["Disappearance of nucleolus","Chromosome movement","Synapsis","Spindle fibres"],c:2,s:`Nucleolus disappears, chromosomes move and spindle fibres form in mitosis.
Synapsis (pairing of homologous chromosomes) occurs in zygotene of meiosis I, not in mitosis.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2016-q065",pn:65,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Blood pressure",q:"Blood pressure in the pulmonary artery is -",o:["More than that in the carotid","More than that in the pulmonary vein","Less than that in the venae cavae","Same as that in the aorta"],c:1,s:`Arteries carry blood pumped by the heart under pressure, so pressure is higher in them than in veins.
So the pulmonary artery has higher pressure than the pulmonary vein.
The pulmonary circuit is a low-pressure circuit, so the pulmonary artery pressure is less than in the carotid or aorta.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Body Fluids and Circulation"},{id:"neet-2016-q066",pn:66,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Evolution",st:"Homologous organs",q:"Which of the following structures is homologus to the wing of the birds ?",o:["Wing of a Moth","Hind limb of Rabbit","Flippers of Whale","Dorsal fin of a Shark"],c:2,s:`Homologous organs have the same basic structure and origin but different functions.
A bird wing and a whale flipper are both modified forelimbs.
A moth wing is not a limb, so it is only analogous.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2016-q067",pn:67,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Apomixis",q:"Seed formation without fertilization in following plants involves the process of",o:["Budding","Somatic hybridization","Apomixis","Sporulation"],c:2,s:"Producing seeds without fertilisation is apomixis (agamospermy).",sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2016-q068",pn:68,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Respiratory disorders",q:"Name the chronic respiratory disorder caused mainly by cigarette smoking",o:["Asthma","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Emphysema"],c:3,s:`In emphysema the alveolar walls are damaged, which reduces the surface for gas exchange.
Cigarette smoking is the main cause.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases"},{id:"neet-2016-q069",pn:69,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Spindle attachment",q:"Spindle fibres attach on to",o:["Kinetochore of the chromosomes","Centromere of the chromosomes","Kinetosome of the chromosomes","Telomere of the chromosomes"],c:0,s:`A kinetochore is a disc-shaped protein structure on the centromere of each chromatid.
Spindle fibres attach to it.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2016-q070",pn:70,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Reproductive Health",st:"Amniocentesis",q:"In context of Amniocentesis, which of the following statement is incorrect ?",o:["It is used for prenatal sex determination","It can be used for detection of Down syndrome","It can be used for detection of Cleft palate.","It is usually done when a woman is between 14-16 weeks pregnant"],c:2,s:`Amniocentesis tests foetal cells in the amniotic fluid taken at about 14-16 weeks.
It detects chromosomal disorders (such as Down syndrome) and sex of the foetus.
Cleft palate is a structural defect and cannot be found by this test.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Reproductive Health"},{id:"neet-2016-q071",pn:71,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Modified stems",q:"Stems modified into flat green organs performing the functions of leaves are known as",o:["Phyllodes","Phylloclades","Scales","Cladodes"],c:1,s:`A phylloclade is a stem modified into a flat green organ that does photosynthesis in place of leaves (e.g. Opuntia).
Phyllodes are modified petioles or rachis, not stems.
Note: Cladodes (4) are also green flat stems in some books; the printed key is (2).`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Morphology of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2016-q072",pn:72,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants",st:"Proton gradient in chloroplast",q:"In a chloroplast the highest number of protons are found in",o:["Lumen of thylakoids","Inter membrane space","Antennae complex","Stroma"],c:0,s:`Protons collect inside the thylakoid lumen from photolysis of water and from plastoquinone carrying H\u207A from stroma to lumen.
This gives the proton gradient that drives ATP synthesis, so the lumen has the highest number.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants"},{id:"neet-2016-q073",pn:73,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"The Living World",st:"Nomenclature rules",q:"Nomenclature is governed by certain universal rules. Which one of the following is contrary to the rules of nomenclature ?",o:["The first word in a biological name represents the genus name, and the second is a specific epithet","The names are written in Latin and are italicised","When written by hand, the names are to be underlined","Biological names can be written in any language"],c:3,s:`Biological names follow one universal system and are written in Latin form.
So saying they can be written in any language is against the rules.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"The Living World"},{id:"neet-2016-q074",pn:74,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Prophase I of meiosis",q:"In meiosis crossing over is initiated at :",o:["Leptotene","Zygotene","Diplotene","Pachytene"],c:3,s:`Prophase I stages: leptotene, zygotene (synapsis), pachytene, diplotene, diakinesis.
Crossing over takes place in pachytene, where recombination nodules form between homologues.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2016-q075",pn:75,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Vaccination",q:"Antivenom injection contains preformed antibodies while polio drops that are administered into the body contain -",o:["Harvested antibodies","Gamma globulin","Attenuated pathogens","Activated pathogens"],c:2,s:`Antivenom gives ready-made antibodies (passive immunity).
Oral polio vaccine contains live attenuated (weakened) polio viruses that induce the body to make its own antibodies.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2016-q076",pn:76,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Taq polymerase",q:"The taq polymerase enzyme is obtained from",o:["Thiobacillus ferroxidans","Bacillus subtilis","Pseudomonas putida","Thermus aquaticus"],c:3,s:`Taq polymerase is a heat-stable DNA polymerase used in PCR.
It comes from the thermophilic bacterium Thermus aquaticus.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2016-q077",pn:77,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Haemophilia",q:"Which of the following most appropriately describes haemophilia ?",o:["X-linked recessive gene disorder","Chromosomal disorder","Dominant gene disorder","Recessive gene disorder"],c:0,s:`Haemophilia is caused by a recessive gene on the X chromosome.
The most precise description is X-linked recessive gene disorder.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2016-q078",pn:78,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Papilionaceous corolla",q:"The standard petal of a papilionaceous corolla is also called -",o:["Pappus","Vexillum","Corona","Carina"],c:1,s:`In a papilionaceous corolla the large posterior petal is the standard (vexillum).
The two lateral petals are wings (alae) and the two fused lower petals are the keel (carina).`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Morphology of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2016-q079",pn:79,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Biotechnology and its Applications",st:"Meloidogyne incognita",q:"Which part of the tobacco plant is infected by Meloidogyne incognita ?",o:["Leaf","Stem","Root","Flower"],c:2,s:`Meloidogyne incognita is a nematode that infects the roots and causes root-knot disease.
It is the nematode used in the RNAi story of tobacco roots.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology and its Applications"},{id:"neet-2016-q080",pn:80,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Viroids",q:"Which of the following statements is wrong for viroids ?",o:["They are smaller than viruses","They cause infections","Their RNA is of high molecular weight","They lack a protein coat"],c:2,s:`Viroids are infectious agents smaller than viruses.
They have no protein coat and consist only of a free RNA of low molecular weight.
So 'high molecular weight' is wrong.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2016-q081",pn:81,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Cancer and telomerase",q:"Which of the following statements is not true for cancer cells in relation to mutations ?",o:["Mutations destroy telomerase inhibitor","Mutations inactivate the cell control","Mutations inhibit production of telomerase","Mutations in proto-oncogenes accelerate the cell cycle"],c:2,s:`In cancer cells, mutations destroy the telomerase inhibitor, so telomerase activity goes up.
Control of the cell cycle is lost, and mutated proto-oncogenes speed up division.
So mutations do not inhibit telomerase production.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2016-q082",pn:82,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Animal tissues",q:`Which type of tissue correctly matches with its locations ?
Tissue - Location:`,o:["Areolar tissue - Tendons","Transitional epithelium - Tip of nose","Cuboidal epithelium - Lining of stomach","Smooth muscle - Wall of intestine"],c:3,s:`Areolar tissue lies under the skin and between organs; tendons are dense regular connective tissue.
Transitional epithelium lines the urinary bladder, not the nose.
Cuboidal epithelium is in glands and tubules; the stomach has columnar epithelium.
Smooth muscle forms the wall of the intestine, so (4) is correct.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Structural Organisation in Animals"},{id:"neet-2016-q083",pn:83,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Antagonistic hormones",q:"Which of the following pairs of hormones are not antagonistic (having opposite effects) to each other ?",o:["Insulin - Glucagon","Aldosterone - Atrial Natriuretic Factor","Relaxin - Inhibin","Parathormone - Calcitonin"],c:2,s:`Insulin lowers and glucagon raises blood glucose.
Aldosterone raises blood pressure and ANF lowers it.
Parathormone raises and calcitonin lowers blood calcium.
Relaxin (loosens pelvic ligaments) and inhibin (inhibits FSH) do not oppose each other.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Chemical Coordination and Integration"},{id:"neet-2016-q084",pn:84,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Stomata",q:"Specialised epidermal cells surrounding the guard cells are called -",o:["Subsidiary cells","Bulliform cells","Lenticels","Complementary cells"],c:0,s:`Subsidiary (accessory) cells are the specialised epidermal cells around the guard cells of a stoma.
Bulliform cells are large cells in grass leaves, and lenticels are pores in bark.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2016-q085",pn:85,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Fertilisation site",q:"Fertilization in humans is practically feasible only if -",o:["The ovum and sperms are transported simultaneously to ampullary-isthmic junction of the fallopian tube","The ovum and sperms are transported simultaneously to ampullary-isthmic junction of the cervix","The sperm are transported into cervix within 48 hrs of release of ovum in uterus","The sperm are transported into vagina just after the release of ovum in fallopian tube"],c:0,s:`Fertilisation takes place in the ampullary-isthmic junction of the fallopian tube.
The ovum lives about 2 days after ovulation and sperm about 4-5 days after insemination, so both must reach this junction together.
The cervix has no ampullary-isthmic junction, so (2) is wrong.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2016-q086",pn:86,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Start codon",q:"Which one of the following is the starter codon ?",o:["UGA","UAA","UAG","AUG"],c:2,s:`AUG is the initiation (start) codon, which codes for methionine.
UGA, UAA and UAG are stop codons.
Note: Printed answer 3 (UAG) kept. UAG is a stop codon and AUG (option 4) is the start codon, as the paper's own solution says.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2016-q087",pn:87,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Sewage and BOD",q:"A river with an inflow of domestic sewage rich in organic waste may result in -",o:["Increased population of aquatic food web organisms","An increased production of fish due to biodegradable nutrients","Death of fish due to lack of oxygen","Drying of the river very soon due to algal bloom"],c:2,s:`Organic waste is broken down by microbes that use dissolved oxygen.
BOD rises and dissolved oxygen falls.
Fish die from lack of oxygen.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Ecosystem"},{id:"neet-2016-q088",pn:88,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Evolution",st:"Origin of life",q:`Following are the two statements regarding the origin of life -
(a) The earliest organisms that appeared on the earth were non-green and presumably anaerobes
(b) The first autotrophic organisms were the chemoautotrophs that never released oxygen
Of the above statements which one of the following options is correct ?`,o:["(b) is correct but (a) is false","Both (a) and (b) are correct","Both (a) and (b) are false","(a) is correct but (b) is false"],c:1,s:`The early atmosphere had no free oxygen, so the first organisms were non-green anaerobes: (a) is correct.
The first autotrophs were chemoautotrophs, which did not release oxygen; oxygen came later from photosynthetic organisms: (b) is correct.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2016-q089",pn:89,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Agricultural practices",q:"A system of rotating crops with legume or grass pasture to improve soil structure and fertility is called -",o:["Contour farming","Strip farming","Shifting agriculture","Ley farming"],c:3,s:`Ley farming alternates crops with legume or grass pasture.
The legume fixes nitrogen and the pasture improves soil structure and fertility.
Note: Agriculture chapter is not in the NEET list; placed in Ecosystem as the nearest.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Ecosystem"},{id:"neet-2016-q090",pn:90,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Competitive exclusion",q:"Gause's principle of competitive exclusion states that -",o:["Competition for the same resources excludes species having different food preferences","No two species can occupy the same niche indefinitely for the same limiting resources","Larger organisms exclude smaller ones through competition","More abundant species will exclude the less abundant species through competition"],c:1,s:`Gause's principle: two closely related species competing for the same limiting resources cannot coexist indefinitely.
One is eliminated, unless they occupy different niches.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Organisms and Populations"},{id:"neet-2016-q091",pn:91,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Features of animal groups",q:`Which of the following characteristic features always holds true for the corresponding group of animals?
Feature - Group:`,o:["Viviparous - Mammalia","Possess a mouth with an upper and a lower jaw - Chordata","3-chambered heart with one incompletely divided ventricle - Reptilia","Cartilaginous endoskeleton - Chondrichthyes"],c:3,s:`Some mammals (Platypus, Echidna) lay eggs, so (1) is not always true.
Protochordates and Agnatha have no jaws, so (2) is not always true.
Crocodiles (reptiles) have a 4-chambered heart, so (3) is not always true.
All Chondrichthyes have a cartilaginous endoskeleton.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2016-q092",pn:92,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"GnRH regulation",q:"Change in GnRH pulse frequency in females is controlled by circulating levels of -",o:["estrogen and inhibin","progesterone only","progesterone and inhibin","estrogen and progesterone"],c:3,s:`In females, circulating estrogen and progesterone feed back on the hypothalamus.
They change the pulse frequency of GnRH release.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2016-q093",pn:93,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Microtubules",q:"Microtubules are the constituents of -",o:["Spindle fibres, Centrioles and Cilia","Centrioles, spindle fibres and chromatin","Centrosome, Nucleosome and Centrioles","Cilia, Flagella and Peroxisomes"],c:0,s:`Microtubules (tubulin) form spindle fibres, centrioles, cilia and flagella.
Chromatin, nucleosomes and peroxisomes are not made of microtubules.
Only option (1) lists only microtubule structures.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2016-q094",pn:94,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Mitochondria and chloroplast",q:`Mitochondria and chloroplast are-
(a) semi-autonomous organelles
(b) formed by division of pre existing organelles and they contain DNA but lack protein synthesising machinery
Which one of the following options is correct?`,o:["(b) is true but (a) is false","(a) is true but (b) is false","Both (a) and (b) are false","Both (a) and (b) are correct"],c:1,s:`Both organelles have their own DNA and ribosomes and divide by fission of earlier organelles, so they are semi-autonomous: (a) is true.
They do have protein-synthesising machinery (70S ribosomes), so (b) is false.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2016-q095",pn:95,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Neural Control and Coordination",st:"Photopigment of the eye",q:"Photosensitive compound in human eye is made up of -",o:["Opsin and Retinal","Opsin and Retinol","Transducin and Retinene","Guanosine and Retinol"],c:0,s:"The photopigment rhodopsin is made of the protein opsin and retinal (an aldehyde of vitamin A).",sub:"Zoology",ch:"Neural Control and Coordination"},{id:"neet-2016-q096",pn:96,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Kingdom Protista",q:"Chrysophytes, Euglenoids, Dinoflagellates and Slime moulds are included in the kingdom",o:["Protista","Fungi","Animalia","Monera"],c:0,s:"These are all unicellular (or simple) eukaryotes, so they belong to Kingdom Protista.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2016-q097",pn:97,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Microbes in Human Welfare",st:"Biogas",q:"The primitive prokaryotes responsible for the production of biogas from the dung of ruminant animals include the -",o:["Thermoacidophiles","Methanogens","Eubacteria","Halophiles"],c:1,s:`Methanogens are archaebacteria that produce methane.
They are present in the rumen and in the biogas digester, where they act on cattle dung.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Microbes in Human Welfare"},{id:"neet-2016-q098",pn:98,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Inhibin",q:"Identify the correct statement on 'inhibin' -",o:["Is produced by granulose cells in ovary and inhibits the secretion of FSH","Is produced by granulose cells in ovary and inhibits the secretion of LH","Is produced by nurse cells in testes and inhibits the secretion of LH","Inhibits the secretion of LH, FSH and Prolactin"],c:0,s:`Inhibin is made by granulosa cells of the ovary (and Sertoli/nurse cells in the testis).
It acts on the pituitary and inhibits secretion of FSH only.
So (1) is correct; (2), (3) and (4) wrongly mention LH or prolactin.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2016-q099",pn:99,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Metabolic rate and size",q:"It is much easier for a small animals to run uphill than for a large animal, because -",o:["Smaller animals have a higher metabolic rate","Small animals have a lower O\u2082 requirement","The efficiency of muscles in large animals is less than in the small animals","It is easier to carry a small body weight"],c:0,s:`Small animals have a higher surface-to-volume ratio and a higher metabolic rate per unit mass.
So they can supply energy faster and run uphill more easily.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Locomotion and Movement"},{id:"neet-2016-q100",pn:100,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Monohybrid genotypic ratio",q:"A tall true breeding garden pea plant is crossed with a dwarf true breeding garden pea plant. When the F1 plants were selfed the resulting genotypes were in the ratio of -",o:["1 : 2 : 1 :: Tall heterozygous : Tall homozygous : Dwarf","3 : 1 :: Tall : Dwarf","3 : 1 :: Dwarf : Tall","1 : 2 : 1 :: Tall homozygous : Tall heterozygous : Dwarf"],c:3,s:`TT \xD7 tt gives F1 all Tt.
Selfing Tt \xD7 Tt gives TT : Tt : tt = 1 : 2 : 1.
That is Tall homozygous : Tall heterozygous : Dwarf, option (4).
The phenotypic ratio would be 3 : 1, but the question asks for genotypes.`,sfig:{w:190,h:210,alt:"Punnett square of Tt \xD7 Tt: gametes T and t on each side; cells TT, Tt, Tt, tt; genotypic ratio 1 : 2 : 1",d:[["t",4,16,"Tt \xD7 Tt"],["c",60,44,8],["t",60,48,"T","ms"],["c",100,44,8],["t",100,48,"t","ms"],["c",22,88,8],["t",22,92,"T","ms"],["c",22,132,8],["t",22,136,"t","ms"],["r",40,66,80,88],["l",80,66,80,154],["l",40,110,120,110],["t",60,92,"TT","ms"],["t",100,92,"Tt","ms"],["t",60,136,"Tt","ms"],["t",100,136,"tt","ms"],["t",4,176,"Genotypic ratio"],["t",30,194,"1 : 2 : 1"]]},sub:"Botany",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2016-q101",pn:101,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Ozone depletion",q:"Depletion of which gas in the atmosphere can lead to an increased incidence of skin cancers -",o:["Ozone","Ammonia","Methane","Nitrous oxide"],c:0,s:`The ozone layer in the stratosphere absorbs harmful UV rays.
When ozone is depleted, more UV reaches the earth and damages skin cells, which can cause skin cancer.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Ecosystem"},{id:"neet-2016-q102",pn:102,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Cropland ecosystem",q:"Which one of the following is a characteristic feature of cropland ecosystem ?",o:["Least genetic diversity","Absence of weeds","Ecological succession","Absence of soil organisms"],c:0,s:`A cropland grows one or a few uniform crop varieties, so it has the least genetic diversity.
Weeds and soil organisms are present, and succession is prevented by farming.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Ecosystem"},{id:"neet-2016-q103",pn:103,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Floral features of families",q:"Tricarpellary, syncarpous gynoecium is found in flowers of -",o:["Solanaceae","Fabaceae","Poaceae","Liliaceae"],c:3,s:`Liliaceae has a tricarpellary, syncarpous, superior ovary.
Solanaceae is bicarpellary, Fabaceae has a single carpel (monocarpellary), and Poaceae is monocarpellary with one ovule.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Morphology of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2016-q104",pn:104,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Mineral Nutrition",st:"Macronutrients",q:"In which of the following, all three are macronutrients ?",o:["Iron, copper, molybdenum","Molybdenum, magnesium, manganese","Nitrogen, nickel, phosphorus","Boron, zinc, manganese"],a:"Bonus",s:`Macronutrients: N, P, K, Ca, Mg, S and others needed in large amounts.
(1), (2) and (4) contain micronutrients (Fe, Cu, Mo, Mn, B, Zn).
(3) has N and P but nickel is a micronutrient.
So no option is fully correct.
Note: Printed key is Bonus: every option contains at least one micronutrient.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Mineral Nutrition"},{id:"neet-2016-q105",pn:105,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Bohr effect",q:"Reduction in pH of blood will-",o:["reduce the blood supply to the brain","decrease the affinity of hemoglobin with oxygen","release bicarbonate ions by the liver","reduce the rate of heart beat"],c:1,s:`A fall in blood pH (more H\u207A or CO\u2082) shifts the haemoglobin-oxygen curve to the right (Bohr effect).
Haemoglobin then holds oxygen less tightly, so its affinity for oxygen decreases.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases"},{id:"neet-2016-q106",pn:106,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Muscle contraction",q:"Lack of relaxation between successive stimuli in sustained muscle contraction is known as -",o:["Fatigue","Tetanus","Tonus","Spasm"],c:1,s:`When stimuli arrive so fast that the muscle gets no time to relax, the contraction is sustained.
This is tetanus.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Locomotion and Movement"},{id:"neet-2016-q107",pn:107,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Names of microbial groups",q:"Which one of the following statements is wrong ?",o:["Golden algae are also called desmids","Eubacteria are also called false bacteria","Phycomycetes are also called algal fungi","Cyanobacteria are also called blue-green algae"],c:1,s:`Eubacteria are the 'true bacteria', not false bacteria, so (2) is wrong.
Phycomycetes are algal fungi and cyanobacteria are blue-green algae.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2016-q108",pn:108,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Restriction enzymes",q:"Which of the following is a restriction endonuclease ?",o:["Protease","DNase I","RNase I","Hind II"],c:3,s:`Restriction endonucleases cut DNA at specific recognition sequences.
Hind II is the first one isolated (from Haemophilus influenzae Rd).
Protease, DNase I and RNase I are not restriction enzymes.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2016-q109",pn:109,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Primary succession",q:"Which of the following would appear as the pioneer organisms on bare rocks?",o:["Liverworts","Mosses","Green algae","Lichens"],c:3,s:`On bare rock, lichens come first.
They secrete acids that weather the rock and form a little soil, so mosses can follow.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Ecosystem"},{id:"neet-2016-q110",pn:110,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Transport in Plants",st:"Gas exchange through stomata",q:"Water vapour comes out from the plant leaf through the stomatal opening. Through the same stomatal opening carbon dioxide diffuses into the plant during photosynthesis. Reason out the above statements using one of following options :",o:["Both processes can happen together because the diffusion coefficient of water and CO\u2082 is different.","The above processes happen only during night time.","One process occurs during day time, and the other at night.","Both processes cannot happen simultaneously."],c:0,s:`Both are simple diffusion along their own concentration gradients.
Water vapour moves out and CO\u2082 moves in at the same time.
They do not hinder each other because the gases have different diffusion coefficients.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Transport in Plants"},{id:"neet-2016-q111",pn:111,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Structure of maize grain",q:"Cotyledon of maize grain is called :",o:["Coleorhiza","Coleoptile","Scutellum","Plumule"],c:2,s:`Maize is monocot, with a single shield-shaped cotyledon called the scutellum.
Coleorhiza covers the radicle, coleoptile covers the plumule.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Morphology of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2016-q112",pn:112,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Sphincter of Oddi",q:"Which of the following guards the opening of hepatopancreatic duct into the duodenum?",o:["Ileocaecal valve","Pyloric sphincter","Sphincter of Oddi","Semilunar valve"],c:2,s:`The common bile duct and pancreatic duct join as the hepatopancreatic duct and open into the duodenum.
This opening is guarded by the sphincter of Oddi.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Digestion and Absorption"},{id:"neet-2016-q113",pn:113,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Gastric glands",q:"In the stomach, gastric acid is secreted by the :",o:["Parietal cells","Peptic cells","Acidic cells","Gastrin secreting cells"],c:0,s:`Parietal (oxyntic) cells of the gastric glands secrete HCl and intrinsic factor.
Peptic (chief) cells secrete pepsinogen.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Digestion and Absorption"},{id:"neet-2016-q114",pn:114,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Excretory Products and their Elimination",st:"Urea formation",q:"In mammals, which blood vessel would normally carry largest amount of urea?",o:["Dorsal Aorta","Hepatic Vein","Hepatic Portal Vein","Renal Vein"],c:1,s:`Urea is made in the liver and enters the blood in the hepatic vein.
The renal vein has less urea because the kidney removes it.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Excretory Products and their Elimination"},{id:"neet-2016-q115",pn:115,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Terminology",q:"The term ecosystem was coined by :",o:["A.G. Tansley","E. Haeckel","E. Warming","E.P. Odum"],c:0,s:"The word 'ecosystem' was coined by A.G. Tansley (1935).",sub:"Botany",ch:"Ecosystem"},{id:"neet-2016-q116",pn:116,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Lac operon",q:"Which of the following is required as inducer(s) for the expression of Lac operon?",o:["galactose","lactose","lactose and galactose","glucose"],c:1,s:`Lactose (allolactose) binds the repressor and releases it from the operator.
Then the structural genes are transcribed, so lactose is the inducer.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2016-q117",pn:117,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Microbes in Human Welfare",st:"Microbial products",q:`Which of the following is wrongly matched in the given table?
Microbe - Product - Application:`,o:["Monascus purpureus - Statins - Lowering of blood cholesterol","Streptococcus - Streptokinase - Removal of clot from blood vessel","Clostridium butylicum - Lipase - Removal of oil stains","Trichoderma polysporum - Cyclosporin A - Immunosuppressive drug"],c:2,s:`Monascus gives statins, Streptococcus gives streptokinase and Trichoderma gives cyclosporin A: all correct.
Clostridium butylicum produces butyric acid, while lipase for oil stains comes from Candida lipolytica.
So (3) is wrongly matched.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Microbes in Human Welfare"},{id:"neet-2016-q118",pn:118,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Logistic growth",q:"When does the growth rate of a population following the logistic model equal zero? The logistic model is given as dN/dt = rN(1 - N/K) :",o:["When N nears the carrying capacity of the habitat.","When N/K equals zero.","When death rate is greater than birth rate.","When N/K is exactly one."],c:3,s:`dN/dt = rN(1 - N/K).
If N/K = 1 then dN/dt = rN(1 - 1) = 0.
If N/K = 0, N is zero and the growth rate is also zero but the population does not exist; 'nears' K is only approximately zero.
So the exact condition is N/K = 1.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Organisms and Populations"},{id:"neet-2016-q119",pn:119,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Anther and pollen",q:"Which one of the following statements is not true?",o:["Exine of pollen grains is made up of sporopollenin","Pollen grains of many species cause severe allergies","Stored pollen in liquid nitrogen can be used in the crop breeding programmes","Tapetum helps in the dehiscence of anther"],c:3,s:`(1), (2) and (3) are correct.
The tapetum nourishes the developing pollen grains.
The endothecium helps anther dehiscence, so (4) is not true.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2016-q120",pn:120,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Fertilisation in lower plants",q:"In bryophytes and pteridophytes, transport of male gametes requires :",o:["Insects","Birds","Water","Wind"],c:2,s:`Male gametes (antherozoids) of bryophytes and pteridophytes are motile and flagellated.
They swim in water to reach the egg.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2016-q121",pn:121,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Modifications",q:"Which of the following is not a stem modification?",o:["Thorns of citrus","Tendrils of cucumber","Flattened structures of Opuntia","Pitcher of Nepenthes"],c:3,s:`Citrus thorns, cucumber tendrils and the flat Opuntia stem (phylloclade) are modified stems.
The pitcher of Nepenthes is a modified leaf.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Morphology of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2016-q122",pn:122,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Membrane-bound organelles",q:"Which one of the following cell organelles is enclosed by a single membrane?",o:["Chloroplasts","Lysosomes","Nuclei","Mitochondria"],c:1,s:`Lysosomes have a single membrane.
Chloroplasts, nuclei and mitochondria are enclosed by a double membrane.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2016-q123",pn:123,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Evolution",st:"Analogous organs",q:"Analogous structures are a result of :",o:["Convergent evolution","Shared ancestry","Stabilizing selection","Divergent evolution"],c:0,s:`Analogous structures have the same function but different origin.
They arise when unrelated organisms adapt to similar conditions, which is convergent evolution.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2016-q124",pn:124,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Biomolecule facts",q:"Which one of the following statements is wrong?",o:["Cellulose is a polysaccharide.","Uracil is a pyrimidine.","Glycine is a sulphur containing amino acid.","Sucrose is a disaccharide."],c:2,s:`Cellulose is a polysaccharide, uracil a pyrimidine, sucrose a disaccharide: all true.
Glycine is the simplest amino acid (R = H) and has no sulphur; cysteine and methionine contain sulphur.
So (3) is wrong.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biomolecules (Biology)"},{id:"neet-2016-q125",pn:125,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Stamen",q:"Proximal end of the filament of stamen is attached to the :",o:["Connective","Placenta","Thalamus or petal","Anther"],c:2,s:`A stamen has a filament and an anther.
The proximal (basal) end of the filament is attached to the thalamus or to the petal; the distal end carries the anther.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Morphology of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2016-q126",pn:126,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"DNA fingerprinting techniques",q:"Which of the following is not required for any of the techniques of DNA fingerprinting available at present?",o:["Zinc finger analysis","Restriction enzymes","DNA - DNA hybridization","Polymerase chain reaction"],c:0,s:`DNA fingerprinting uses restriction enzymes, DNA-DNA hybridisation with probes and PCR.
Zinc finger analysis is a protein study and is not part of it.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2016-q127",pn:127,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Birds and mammals",q:"Which one of the following characteristics is not shared by birds and mammals?",o:["Breathing using lungs","Viviparity","Warm blooded nature","Ossified endoskeleton"],c:1,s:`Birds and mammals both breathe with lungs, are warm-blooded and have a bony endoskeleton.
Birds are always oviparous, while most mammals are viviparous, so viviparity is not shared.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2016-q128",pn:128,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Hormones in the menstrual cycle",q:"Select the incorrect statement :",o:["LH triggers ovulation in ovary.","LH and FSH decrease gradually during the follicular phase.","LH triggers secretion of androgens from the Leydig cells.","FSH stimulates the sertoli cells which help in spermiogenesis."],c:1,s:`A surge in LH triggers ovulation; LH acts on Leydig cells for androgens; FSH acts on Sertoli cells which help spermiogenesis.
During the follicular phase LH and FSH rise gradually, they do not decrease.
So (2) is incorrect.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2016-q129",pn:129,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Hormone precursors",q:"The amino acid Tryptophan is the precursor for the synthesis of :",o:["Thyroxine and Triiodothyronine","Estrogen and Progesterone","Cortisol and Cortisone","Melatonin and Serotonin"],c:3,s:`Melatonin and serotonin are made from tryptophan.
Thyroid hormones come from tyrosine, and the steroid hormones come from cholesterol.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Chemical Coordination and Integration"},{id:"neet-2016-q130",pn:130,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Joint Forest Management",q:"Joint Forest Management Concept was introduced in India during :",o:["1970s","1980s","1990s","1960s"],c:1,s:"Joint Forest Management, in which local communities help protect forests, was introduced in India in the 1980s.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Biodiversity and Conservation"},{id:"neet-2016-q131",pn:131,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Fungal cell wall",q:"One of the major components of cell wall of most fungi is :",o:["Peptidoglycan","Cellulose","Hemicellulose","Chitin"],c:3,s:`Most fungal cell walls contain chitin and polysaccharides.
Peptidoglycan is in bacteria and cellulose in plants.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2016-q132",pn:132,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Polysome",q:"A complex of ribosomes attached to a single strand of RNA is known as :",o:["Polymer","Polypeptide","Okazaki fragment","Polysome"],c:3,s:"Many ribosomes reading one mRNA at the same time form a string called a polysome (polyribosome).",sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2016-q133",pn:133,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Phylum Arthropoda",q:"Which of the following features is not present in the Phylum - Arthropoda?",o:["Metameric segmentation","Parapodia","Jointed appendages","Chitinous exoskeleton"],c:1,s:`Arthropods have a segmented body, jointed appendages and a chitinous exoskeleton.
Parapodia are fleshy paddle-like outgrowths of polychaete annelids such as Nereis.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2016-q134",pn:134,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Asthma",q:"Asthma may be attributed to :",o:["allergic reaction of the mast cells in the lungs","inflammation of the trachea","accumulation of fluid in the lungs","bacterial infection of the lungs"],c:0,s:`Asthma is an allergic disorder: mast cells in the lungs release histamine, which inflames and narrows the airways.
Other options describe different conditions (tracheitis, pulmonary oedema, pneumonia).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2016-q135",pn:135,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Genetic disorders",q:`Pick out the correct statements :
(a) Haemophilia is a sex-linked recessive disease.
(b) Down's syndrome is due to aneuploidy.
(c) Phenylketonuria is an autosomal recessive gene disorder.
(d) Sickle cell anaemia is an X - linked recessive gene disorder.`,o:["(b) and (d) are correct.","(a), (c) and (d) are correct.","(a), (b) and (c) are correct.","(a) and (d) are correct."],c:2,s:`(a) Haemophilia is X-linked recessive: correct.
(b) Down's syndrome is trisomy 21, a kind of aneuploidy: correct.
(c) Phenylketonuria is autosomal recessive: correct.
(d) Sickle cell anaemia is autosomal recessive, not X-linked: wrong.
So (a), (b) and (c): option (3).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2016-q136",pn:136,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Energy loss on sharing charge",q:"A capacitor of 2 \u03BCF is charged as shown in the diagram. When the switch S is turned to position 2, the percentage of its stored energy dissipated is :",o:["20 %","75 %","80 %","0 %"],c:2,s:`Initial energy U = \xBD(2 \u03BCF)V\xB2 = V\xB2 (in \u03BCF units).
After the switch moves, charge CV = 2V is shared by 2 \u03BCF and the uncharged 8 \u03BCF capacitor.
Common voltage V' = 2V/(2 + 8) = V/5.
Final energy = \xBD(10)(V/5)\xB2 = V\xB2/5.
Energy lost = V\xB2 - V\xB2/5 = 4V\xB2/5.
Percentage dissipated = (4V\xB2/5)/V\xB2 \xD7 100 = 80 %.`,fig:{w:230,h:175,alt:"Circuit: battery V and 2 \u03BCF capacitor on the left branches, 8 \u03BCF capacitor on the right, two-way switch S with contacts 1 and 2 at the top, set to 1.",d:[["l",37,28,87,28],["l",128,28,185,28],["l",37,28,37,92],["l",37,102,37,155],["l",37,155,185,155],["l",185,155,185,98],["l",185,28,185,86],["c",90,28,3],["c",125,28,3],["l",112,64,92,32,"a"],["c",112,64,2.5],["l",112,66,112,88],["l",102,88,122,88],["l",102,96,122,96],["l",112,96,112,155],["c",112,155,2,"F"],["l",28,92,46,92],["l",33,100,41,100],["t",12,100,"V","m"],["l",175,86,195,86],["l",175,94,195,94],["l",185,94,185,98],["t",88,20,"1","m"],["t",125,20,"2","m"],["t",120,67,"S"],["t",124,116,"2 \u03BCF"],["t",200,88,"8 \u03BCF"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance"},{id:"neet-2016-q137",pn:137,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"Logic gates",q:"To get output 1 for the following circuit, the correct choice for the input is :",o:["A = 1, B = 0, C = 0","A = 1, B = 1, C = 0","A = 1, B = 0, C = 1","A = 0, B = 1, C = 0"],c:2,s:`Inputs A and B go into an OR gate; its output and input C go into an AND gate.
Y = (A + B)\xB7C.
Y = 1 needs C = 1 and (A or B) = 1.
Only A = 1, B = 0, C = 1 satisfies this.`,fig:{w:260,h:80,alt:"Logic circuit: A and B feed an OR gate; its output and C feed an AND gate whose output is Y.",d:[["c",22,16,2.5,"F"],["c",22,36,2.5,"F"],["c",22,56,2.5,"F"],["t",8,20,"A","m"],["t",8,40,"B","m"],["t",8,60,"C","m"],["l",22,16,75,16],["l",22,36,75,36],["p","M70 6C90 6 102 14 112 26C102 38 90 46 70 46C78 33 78 19 70 6Z"],["l",112,26,177,26],["l",22,56,177,56],["p","M177 16H205A25 25 0 0 1 205 66H177Z"],["l",230,41,245,41],["c",245,41,2.5,"F"],["t",254,45,"Y","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Semiconductor Electronics"},{id:"neet-2016-q138",pn:138,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Current Electricity",st:"Potentiometer",q:"A potentiometer wire is 100 cm long and a constant potential difference is maintained across it. Two cells are connected in series first to support one another and then in opposite direction. The balance points are obtained at 50 cm and 10 cm from the positive end of the wire in the two cases. The ratio of emf's is :",o:["5 : 4","3 : 4","3 : 2","5 : 1"],c:2,s:`Potential gradient per cm = x.
Supporting: E\u2081 + E\u2082 = 50x.
Opposing: E\u2081 - E\u2082 = 10x.
Divide: (E\u2081 + E\u2082)/(E\u2081 - E\u2082) = 5/1.
So E\u2081 + E\u2082 = 5E\u2081 - 5E\u2082, so 4E\u2081 = 6E\u2082.
E\u2081/E\u2082 = 3/2.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Current Electricity"},{id:"neet-2016-q139",pn:139,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"Photoelectric effect",q:"When a metallic surface is illuminated with radiation of wavelength \u03BB, the stopping potential is V. If the same surface is illuminated with radiation of wavelength 2\u03BB, the stopping potential is V/4. The threshold wavelength for the metallic surface is :",o:["5\u03BB","(5/2)\u03BB","3\u03BB","4\u03BB"],c:2,s:`eV = hc/\u03BB - W ...(i)
eV/4 = hc/2\u03BB - W ...(ii)
Multiply (ii) by 4: eV = 2hc/\u03BB - 4W.
Subtract from (i): 0 = hc/\u03BB - 2hc/\u03BB - W + 4W = -hc/\u03BB + 3W.
So W = hc/3\u03BB.
But W = hc/\u03BB\u2080, so \u03BB\u2080 = 3\u03BB.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter"},{id:"neet-2016-q140",pn:140,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Floating in two liquids",q:"Two non-mixing liquids of densities \u03C1 and n\u03C1 (n > 1) are put in a container. The height of each liquid is h. A solid cylinder of length L and density d is put in this container. The cylinder floats with its axis vertical and length pL (p < 1) in the denser liquid. The density d is equal to :",o:["{2 + (n + 1)p}\u03C1","{2 + (n - 1)p}\u03C1","{1 + (n - 1)p}\u03C1","{1 + (n + 1)p}\u03C1"],c:2,s:`Let the area of cross-section be A.
The length pL is in the denser liquid (n\u03C1) and (1 - p)L is in the lighter liquid (\u03C1).
Weight = buoyant force: LAdg = (1 - p)LA\u03C1g + pLA(n\u03C1)g.
d = (1 - p)\u03C1 + np\u03C1 = \u03C1{1 + (n - 1)p}.`,sfig:{w:290,h:295,alt:"Cylinder of length L across two liquids: (1 - p)L in density \u03C1 above, pL in density n\u03C1 below; buoyant forces up, weight LAdg down.",d:[["r",92,83,82,142],["l",13,153,92,153],["l",174,153,260,153],["l",132,120,132,38,"a"],["l",132,120,132,66,"a"],["l",132,187,132,262,"a"],["l",62,150,62,83,"a"],["l",62,156,62,225,"a"],["t",132,22,"(1\u2013p)LA\u03C1g","m"],["t",140,58,"pLAn\u03C1g"],["t",58,121,"(1\u2013p)L","e"],["t",58,196,"pL","e"],["t",205,136,"\u03C1"],["t",205,196,"n\u03C1"],["t",132,282,"LAdg","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids"},{id:"neet-2016-q141",pn:141,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"Source of EM waves",q:"Out of the following options which one can be used to produce a propagating electromagnetic wave ?",o:["A stationary charge","A chargeless particle","An accelerating charges","A charge moving at constant velocity"],c:2,s:`A stationary charge gives only an electric field; a charge at constant velocity gives a steady magnetic field as well but no radiation.
A chargeless particle makes no field.
An accelerating charge produces changing electric and magnetic fields that propagate as an EM wave.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electromagnetic Waves"},{id:"neet-2016-q142",pn:142,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Current Electricity",st:"Heat from time-varying charge",q:"The charge flowing through a resistance R varies with time t as Q = at - bt\xB2, where a and b are positive constants. The total heat produced in R is :",o:["a\xB3R/3b","a\xB3R/2b","a\xB3R/b","a\xB3R/6b"],c:3,s:`I = dQ/dt = a - 2bt.
I becomes zero at t = a/2b.
Heat = \u222BI\xB2R dt from 0 to a/2b = R\u222B(a - 2bt)\xB2 dt = R\u222B(a\xB2 - 4abt + 4b\xB2t\xB2) dt.
= R[a\xB2t - 2abt\xB2 + 4b\xB2t\xB3/3] from 0 to a/2b.
At t = a/2b: a\xB2t = a\xB3/2b, 2abt\xB2 = a\xB3/2b, 4b\xB2t\xB3/3 = a\xB3/6b.
Heat = R(a\xB3/2b - a\xB3/2b + a\xB3/6b) = a\xB3R/6b.
Note: Printed answer 4 (a\xB3R/6b) integrates only to t = a/2b where I = 0; if the charge is followed until Q returns to zero (t = a/b) the integral is a\xB3R/3b, but the printed key is kept.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Current Electricity"},{id:"neet-2016-q143",pn:143,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Potential and field at height",q:"At what height from the surface of earth the gravitation potential and the value of g are -5.4 \xD7 10\u2077 J kg\u207B\xB2 and 6.0 m s\u207B\xB2 respectively ? Take the radius of earth as 6400 km :",o:["1600 km","1400 km","2000 km","2600 km"],c:3,s:`At distance r = R + h from the centre: V = -GM/r and g = GM/r\xB2.
So |V|/g = r.
r = 5.4 \xD7 10\u2077/6.0 = 9 \xD7 10\u2076 m = 9000 km.
h = r - R = 9000 - 6400 = 2600 km.
Note: Unit of potential is printed J kg\u207B\xB2 in the paper; it should be J kg\u207B\xB9.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Gravitation"},{id:"neet-2016-q144",pn:144,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Thermal Properties of Matter",st:"Thermal expansion",q:"Coefficient of linear expansion of brass and steel rods are \u03B1\u2081 and \u03B1\u2082. Lengths of brass and steel rods are l\u2081 and l\u2082 respectively. If (l\u2082 - l\u2081) is maintained same at all temperatures, which one of the following relations holds good ?",o:["\u03B1\u2081l\u2082\xB2 = \u03B1\u2082l\u2081\xB2","\u03B1\u2081\xB2l\u2082 = \u03B1\u2082\xB2l\u2081","\u03B1\u2081l\u2081 = \u03B1\u2082l\u2082","\u03B1\u2081l\u2082 = \u03B1\u2082l\u2081"],c:2,s:`Rise in length of brass = l\u2081\u03B1\u2081\u0394T and of steel = l\u2082\u03B1\u2082\u0394T.
The difference (l\u2082 - l\u2081) stays the same only if both rods expand by the same amount.
So l\u2081\u03B1\u2081\u0394T = l\u2082\u03B1\u2082\u0394T, giving \u03B1\u2081l\u2081 = \u03B1\u2082l\u2082.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Thermal Properties of Matter"},{id:"neet-2016-q145",pn:145,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Intensity in Young's experiment",q:"The intensity at the maximum in a Young's double slit experiment is I\u2080. Distance between two slits is d = 5\u03BB, where \u03BB is the wavelength of light used in the experiment. What will be the intensity in front of one of the slits on the screen placed at a distance D = 10 d ?",o:["I\u2080/4","(3/4)I\u2080","I\u2080/2","I\u2080"],c:2,s:`D = 10d = 50\u03BB and d = 5\u03BB.
Take the point P in front of S\u2081. Then S\u2081P = D = 50\u03BB and S\u2082P = \u221A(D\xB2 + d\xB2) = \u221A((50\u03BB)\xB2 + (5\u03BB)\xB2).
Path difference = \u03BB(\u221A2525 - 50) \u2248 0.25\u03BB.
Phase difference \u03C6 = (2\u03C0/\u03BB) \xD7 0.25\u03BB = \u03C0/2.
With equal intensities from both slits, I = I\u2080cos\xB2(\u03C6/2) = I\u2080cos\xB2(\u03C0/4) = I\u2080/2.`,sfig:{w:300,h:265,alt:"Slits S1 and S2 a distance d = 5\u03BB apart on the left; screen at D = 10d = 50\u03BB with P in front of S1; S2P slants up to P.",d:[["l",62,2,62,258],["l",263,2,263,258],["l",62,71,263,71],["l",62,128,263,128],["l",62,192,263,71],["l",38,112,38,82,"a"],["l",38,146,38,180,"a"],["t",40,74,"S\u2081","m"],["t",40,200,"S\u2082","m"],["t",36,132,"d = 5\u03BB","m"],["t",160,60,"D = 10d = 50\u03BB","m"],["t",272,75,"P"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Wave Optics"},{id:"neet-2016-q146",pn:146,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Atoms",st:"Balmer series limit",q:"Given the value of Rydberg constant is 10\u2077 m\u207B\xB9, the wave number of the last line of the Balmer series in hydrogen spectrum will be :",o:["0.5 \xD7 10\u2077 m\u207B\xB9","0.25 \xD7 10\u2077 m\u207B\xB9","2.5 \xD7 10\u2077 m\u207B\xB9","0.025 \xD7 10\u2074 m\u207B\xB9"],c:1,s:`Wave number = 1/\u03BB = RZ\xB2(1/n\u2081\xB2 - 1/n\u2082\xB2).
Balmer series: n\u2081 = 2; the last line has n\u2082 = \u221E.
1/\u03BB = R(1)\xB2(1/4 - 0) = R/4.
= 10\u2077/4 = 0.25 \xD7 10\u2077 m\u207B\xB9.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Atoms"},{id:"neet-2016-q147",pn:147,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Escape velocity and density",q:"The ratio of escape velocity at earth (v\u2091) to the escape velocity at a planet (v\u209A) whose radius and mean density are twice as that of earth is :",o:["1 : 2\u221A2","1 : 4","1 : \u221A2","1 : 2"],c:0,s:`v\u2091(escape) = \u221A(2GM/R) and M = (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3\u03C1, so v = \u221A((8/3)\u03C0G\u03C1R\xB2) = R\u221A((8/3)\u03C0G\u03C1).
v \u221D R\u221A\u03C1.
v\u2091/v\u209A = (R\u2091/R\u209A)\u221A(\u03C1\u2091/\u03C1\u209A) = (1/2)\u221A(1/2) = 1/(2\u221A2).
So the ratio is 1 : 2\u221A2.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Gravitation"},{id:"neet-2016-q148",pn:148,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Electromagnetic Induction",st:"Self inductance",q:"A long solenoid has 1000 turns. When a current of 4A flows through it, the magnetic flux linked with each turn of the solenoid is 4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 Wb. The self-inductance of the solenoid is :",o:["3 H","2 H","1 H","4 H"],c:2,s:`Self-inductance L = N\u03C6/I.
L = (1000 \xD7 4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)/4 = 1 H.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electromagnetic Induction"},{id:"neet-2016-q149",pn:149,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Laws of Motion",st:"Banked road with friction",q:"A car is negotiating a curved road of radius R. The road is banked at an angle \u03B8. The coefficient of friction between the tyres of the car and the road is \u03BC\u209B. The maximum safe velocity on this road is :",o:["\u221A(gR (\u03BC\u209B + tan\u03B8)/(1 - \u03BC\u209Btan\u03B8))","\u221A((g/R)(\u03BC\u209B + tan\u03B8)/(1 - \u03BC\u209Btan\u03B8))","\u221A((g/R\xB2)(\u03BC\u209B + tan\u03B8)/(1 - \u03BC\u209Btan\u03B8))","\u221A(gR\xB2(\u03BC\u209B + tan\u03B8)/(1 - \u03BC\u209Btan\u03B8))"],c:0,s:`At maximum speed friction \u03BC\u209BN acts down the slope.
Horizontal (towards centre): N sin\u03B8 + \u03BC\u209BN cos\u03B8 = mv\xB2/R ...(i)
Vertical: N cos\u03B8 = mg + \u03BC\u209BN sin\u03B8, so N cos\u03B8 - \u03BC\u209BN sin\u03B8 = mg ...(ii)
Divide (i) by (ii): (sin\u03B8 + \u03BC\u209Bcos\u03B8)/(cos\u03B8 - \u03BC\u209Bsin\u03B8) = v\xB2/Rg.
Divide top and bottom by cos\u03B8: (tan\u03B8 + \u03BC\u209B)/(1 - \u03BC\u209Btan\u03B8) = v\xB2/Rg.
v = \u221A(Rg(\u03BC\u209B + tan\u03B8)/(1 - \u03BC\u209Btan\u03B8)).`,fig:{w:340,h:285,alt:"Car on a banked road of angle \u03B8 with forces N, N cos\u03B8, N sin\u03B8, \u03BC\u209BN, \u03BC\u209BN cos\u03B8, \u03BC\u209BN sin\u03B8 and mg.",d:[["l",20,68,20,230],["l",20,230,242,230,"a"],["l",20,68,230,248,"a"],["p","M130 60L188 110L152 154L94 104Z"],["c",101,124,10,"F"],["c",133,152,10,"F"],["l",130,110,130,30,"a"],["l",130,110,186,50,"a"],["l",130,110,74,168,"a"],["l",130,110,214,110,"a"],["l",130,141,258,141,"a"],["l",130,154,130,196,"a"],["p","M130 78A32 32 0 0 1 153 87"],["p","M110 130A28 28 0 0 0 130 138"],["p","M158 141A28 28 0 0 1 151 158"],["p","M179 230A30 30 0 0 1 186 211"],["t",130,20,"Ncos\u03B8","m"],["t",190,50,"N"],["t",148,70,"\u03B8"],["t",222,114,"Nsin\u03B8"],["t",264,145,"\u03BC\u209BNcos\u03B8"],["t",70,178,"\u03BC\u209BNsin\u03B8","e"],["t",130,210,"mg","m"],["t",104,150,"\u03B8","e"],["t",148,174,"\u03B8"],["t",160,214,"\u03B8","e"],["t",216,264,"\u03BC\u209BN"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Laws of Motion"},{id:"neet-2016-q150",pn:150,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Magnetism and Matter",st:"Susceptibility",q:"The magnetic susceptibility is negative for :",o:["paramagnetic material only","ferromagnetic material only","paramagnetic and ferromagnetic materials","diamagnetic material only"],c:3,s:`Paramagnetic and ferromagnetic materials have positive susceptibility.
Diamagnetic materials are weakly repelled by a magnetic field, so their susceptibility is negative.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Magnetism and Matter"},{id:"neet-2016-q151",pn:151,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Waves",st:"Doppler effect and echo",q:"A siren emitting a sound of frequency 800 Hz moves away from an observer towards a cliff at a speed of 15 m s\u207B\xB9. Then, the frequency of sound that the observer hears in the echo reflected from the cliff is : (Take velocity of sound in air = 330 m s\u207B\xB9)",o:["800 Hz","838 Hz","885 Hz","765 Hz"],c:1,s:`The cliff is at rest, so it receives sound from a source moving towards it.
Frequency received by the cliff: n' = n\xB7v/(v - v_s).
The cliff reflects this frequency, and the observer (at rest) hears it unchanged.
n' = 800 \xD7 330/(330 - 15) = 800 \xD7 330/315 = 838 Hz.`,sfig:{w:330,h:145,alt:"Observer at left, source on a cart moving right with speed v\u209B towards a cliff (v_R = 0); sound n goes to the cliff and the echo returns to the observer.",d:[["l",12,129,221,129],["l",215,30,215,129],["l",215,36,221,30],["l",215,42,221,36],["l",215,48,221,42],["l",215,54,221,48],["l",215,60,221,54],["l",215,66,221,60],["l",215,72,221,66],["l",215,78,221,72],["l",215,84,221,78],["l",215,90,221,84],["l",215,96,221,90],["l",215,102,221,96],["l",215,108,221,102],["l",215,114,221,108],["l",215,120,221,114],["l",215,126,221,120],["c",33,103,8],["l",33,111,33,121],["l",23,116,43,116],["l",33,121,24,129],["l",33,121,43,129],["r",78,98,40,16],["t",98,110,"source","ms"],["c",86,122,7],["c",110,122,7],["l",118,106,138,106,"a"],["t",141,110,"v\u209B"],["t",157,126,"v\u209B"],["p","M213 77C160 48 70 55 37 89","a"],["p","M102 92C130 75 175 72 211 80","a"],["p","M92 90C75 84 58 85 43 92","a"],["t",193,20,"v\u1D63 = 0"],["t",28,78,"n\u2034"],["t",97,87,"n","m"],["t",47,106,"n\u2033"],["t",200,58,"n\u2033","m"],["t",204,94,"n\u2032","m"],["t",228,68,"n\u2033 ="],["t",260,73,"n","s"],["p","M272 50C268 62 268 76 272 88"],["p","M314 50C318 62 318 76 314 88"],["l",277,69,310,69],["t",293,63,"v","m"],["t",293,83,"v \u2212 v\u209B","ms"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Waves"},{id:"neet-2016-q152",pn:152,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Instantaneous power",q:"A body of mass 1 kg begins to move under the action of a time dependent force F = (2t \xEE + 3t\xB2 \u0135) N, where \xEE and \u0135 are unit vectors along x and y axis. What power will be developed by the force at the time t ?",o:["(2t\xB2 + 4t\u2074) W","(2t\xB3 + 3t\u2074) W","(2t\xB3 + 3t\u2075) W","(2t\xB2 + 3t\xB3) W"],c:2,s:`a = F/m = 2t \xEE + 3t\xB2 \u0135 (m = 1 kg).
v = \u222Ba dt = t\xB2 \xEE + t\xB3 \u0135 (starts from rest).
P = F\xB7v = (2t)(t\xB2) + (3t\xB2)(t\xB3).
P = (2t\xB3 + 3t\u2075) W.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Work, Energy and Power"},{id:"neet-2016-q153",pn:153,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Moment of inertia of a disc with a hole",q:"From a disc of radius R and mass M, a circular hole of diameter R, whose rim passes through the centre is cut. What is the moment of inertia of the remaining part of the disc about a perpendicular axis, passing through the centre ?",o:["13 MR\xB2/32","11 MR\xB2/32","9 MR\xB2/32","15 MR\xB2/32"],c:0,s:`Mass of the hole: M' = (M/\u03C0R\xB2) \xD7 \u03C0(R/2)\xB2 = M/4.
Moment of inertia of the full disc about the axis: I = MR\xB2/2.
The hole has radius R/2 and its centre is R/2 from the axis.
By the parallel axis theorem: I' = (M/4)(R/2)\xB2/2 + (M/4)(R/2)\xB2 = MR\xB2/32 + MR\xB2/16 = 3MR\xB2/32.
Remaining part: I - I' = MR\xB2/2 - 3MR\xB2/32 = 13MR\xB2/32.`,sfig:{w:170,h:190,alt:"Disc of radius R with vertical axis XX\u2032 through its centre; a hole of radius R/2 whose rim passes through the centre lies to the right of the axis.",d:[["c",67,92,42],["c",88,92,21],["l",67,12,67,176],["l",67,92,109,92],["l",88,88,88,96],["t",58,24,"X","m"],["t",58,168,"X\u2032","m"],["t",88,82,"R/2","ms"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion"},{id:"neet-2016-q154",pn:154,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Single slit diffraction",q:"In a diffraction pattern due to a single slit of width 'a', the first minimum is observed at an angle 30\xBA when light of wavelength 5000 \xC5 is incident on the slit. The first secondary maximum is observed at an angle of :",o:["sin\u207B\xB9(2/3)","sin\u207B\xB9(1/2)","sin\u207B\xB9(3/4)","sin\u207B\xB9(1/4)"],c:2,s:`First minimum: a sin30\xBA = \u03BB, so \u03BB/a = 1/2.
First secondary maximum: a sin\u03B8 = 3\u03BB/2.
sin\u03B8 = (3/2)(\u03BB/a) = (3/2)(1/2) = 3/4.
\u03B8 = sin\u207B\xB9(3/4).`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Wave Optics"},{id:"neet-2016-q155",pn:155,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Moving Charges and Magnetism",st:"Force on a current loop near a wire",q:"A square loop ABCD carrying a current i is placed near and coplanar with a long straight conductor XY carrying a current I, the net force on the loop will be :",o:["\u03BC\u2080Ii/2\u03C0","2\u03BC\u2080IiL/3\u03C0","\u03BC\u2080IiL/2\u03C0","2\u03BC\u2080Ii/3\u03C0"],c:3,s:`The forces on the two sides parallel to the wire (AB at distance L/2, CD at distance 3L/2) act in opposite directions; the forces on BC and AD cancel.
F_AB = (\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0(L/2)) \xD7 i \xD7 L = \u03BC\u2080Ii/\u03C0.
F_CD = (\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0(3L/2)) \xD7 i \xD7 L = \u03BC\u2080Ii/3\u03C0.
Net force = \u03BC\u2080Ii/\u03C0 - \u03BC\u2080Ii/3\u03C0 = 2\u03BC\u2080Ii/3\u03C0.`,fig:{w:190,h:145,alt:"Long wire XY carrying current I upward, with square loop ABCD of side L at distance L/2 carrying current i.",d:[["l",34,21,34,105],["l",34,66,34,52,"a"],["r",65,21,74,74],["p","M108 72C98 72 92 66 92 58C92 52 96 49 100 47","a"],["t",103,66,"i"],["l",163,21,163,95,"b"],["l",159,21,167,21],["l",159,95,167,95],["l",34,117,65,117,"b"],["l",65,117,138,117,"b"],["l",34,113,34,121],["l",65,113,65,121],["l",138,113,138,121],["t",67,16,"B","m"],["t",142,16,"C","m"],["t",25,32,"Y","m"],["t",23,66,"I","m"],["t",24,104,"X","m"],["t",68,110,"A","m"],["t",138,110,"D","m"],["t",172,63,"L","m"],["t",50,134,"L/2","m"],["t",101,132,"L","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Moving Charges and Magnetism"},{id:"neet-2016-q156",pn:156,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Thermal Properties of Matter",st:"Wien's displacement law",q:"A black body is at a temperature of 5760 K. The energy of radiation emitted by the body at wavelength 250 nm is U\u2081, at wavelength 500 nm is U\u2082 and that at 1000 nm is U\u2083. Wien's constant, b = 2.88 \xD7 10\u2076 nm K. Which of the following is correct ?",o:["U\u2083 = 0","U\u2081 > U\u2082","U\u2082 > U\u2081","U\u2081 = 0"],c:2,s:`Wien's law: \u03BB_m T = b.
\u03BB_m = 2.88 \xD7 10\u2076/5760 = 500 nm.
The emitted energy is greatest at 500 nm, so U\u2082 is larger than both U\u2081 and U\u2083.
So U\u2082 > U\u2081.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Thermal Properties of Matter"},{id:"neet-2016-q157",pn:157,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Waves",st:"Resonance in a closed pipe",q:"An air column, closed at one end and open at the other, resonates with a tuning fork when the smallest length of the column is 50 cm. The next larger length of the column resonating with the same tuning fork is :",o:["100 cm","150 cm","200 cm","66.7 cm"],c:1,s:`A closed pipe resonates at lengths l = \u03BB/4, 3\u03BB/4, 5\u03BB/4 ...
Smallest: l\u2081 = \u03BB/4 = 50 cm.
Next: l\u2082 = 3\u03BB/4 = 3 \xD7 50 = 150 cm.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Waves"},{id:"neet-2016-q158",pn:158,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Kinetic Theory",st:"RMS speed and temperature",q:"The molecules of a given mass of a gas have r.m.s velocity of 200 m s\u207B\xB9 at 27\xBAC and 1.0 \xD7 10\u2075 N m\u207B\xB2 pressure. When the temperature and pressure of the gas are respectively, 127\xBAC and 0.05 \xD7 10\u2075 N m\u207B\xB2, the r.m.s. velocity of its molecules in m s\u207B\xB9 is :",o:["400/\u221A3","100\u221A2/3","100/3","100\u221A2"],c:0,s:`v_rms depends only on temperature (v \u221D \u221AT) for a given gas, not on pressure.
T\u2081 = 27 + 273 = 300 K, T\u2082 = 127 + 273 = 400 K.
v\u2082/v\u2081 = \u221A(400/300) = 2/\u221A3.
v\u2082 = 200 \xD7 2/\u221A3 = 400/\u221A3 m s\u207B\xB9.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Kinetic Theory"},{id:"neet-2016-q159",pn:159,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"Ideal diode circuit",q:"Consider the junction diode as ideal. The value of current flowing through AB is :",o:["10\u207B\xB2 A","10\u207B\xB9 A","10\u207B\xB3 A","0 A"],c:0,s:`The p-side (A) is at +4 V and the n-side (towards B) is at -6 V, so the diode is forward biased.
An ideal forward-biased diode has zero resistance.
Potential difference = 4 - (-6) = 10 V across the 1 k\u03A9 resistor.
I = 10/1000 = 10\u207B\xB2 A.`,fig:{w:285,h:65,alt:"Circuit: terminal A at +4 V, ideal diode pointing from A to B, 1 k\u03A9 resistor, terminal B at \u22126 V.",d:[["c",28,28,2.5,"F"],["c",253,28,2.5,"F"],["l",28,28,92,28],["p","M92 19L92 37L106 28Z"],["l",106,19,106,37],["l",106,28,145,28],["p","M145 28L147.4 33L152.1 23L156.9 33L161.6 23L166.4 33L171.1 23L175.9 33L180.6 23L183 28"],["l",183,28,253,28],["t",28,16,"A","m"],["t",253,16,"B","m"],["t",164,16,"1 k\u03A9","m"],["t",28,52,"+4 V","m"],["t",253,52,"\u20136 V","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Semiconductor Electronics"},{id:"neet-2016-q160",pn:160,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Motion in a Plane",st:"Vector addition",q:"If the magnitude of sum of two vectors is equal to the magnitude of difference of the two vectors, the angle between these vectors is :",o:["90\xBA","45\xBA","180\xBA","0\xBA"],c:0,s:`|A + B| = |A - B|.
\u221A(A\xB2 + B\xB2 + 2AB cos\u03B8) = \u221A(A\xB2 + B\xB2 - 2AB cos\u03B8).
Square both sides: 4AB cos\u03B8 = 0.
So cos\u03B8 = 0 and \u03B8 = 90\xBA.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Motion in a Plane"},{id:"neet-2016-q161",pn:161,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments",st:"Astronomical telescope",q:"An astronomical telescope has objective and eyepiece of focal lengths 40 cm and 4 cm respectively. To view an object 200 cm away from the objective, the lenses must be separated by a distance :",o:["46.0 cm","50.0 cm","54.0 cm","37.3 cm"],c:2,s:`Objective: u = -200 cm, f = 40 cm.
1/v = 1/f + 1/u = 1/40 - 1/200 = 4/200 = 1/50, so v\u2080 = 50 cm.
For the final image at infinity (relaxed eye) the objective image lies at the eyepiece focus, so u_e = 4 cm.
Separation = 50 + 4 = 54 cm.`,sfig:{w:375,h:158,alt:"Object AB 200 cm left of the objective (f\u2080 = 40 cm); image B\u2032 at V\u2080 to the right; eyepiece (f\u2091 = 4 cm) at U\u2091 beyond B\u2032.",d:[["l",24,84,354,84],["l",24,84,24,61.5,"a"],["p","M127.5 39C139.5 60 139.5 108 127.5 130.5C115.5 108 115.5 60 127.5 39Z"],["p","M301.5 39C313.5 60 313.5 108 301.5 130.5C289.5 108 289.5 60 301.5 39Z"],["l",39,60,27,60,"a"],["t",72,66,"200 cm","m"],["l",106.5,60,120,60,"a"],["l",142.5,60,135,60,"a"],["t",162,67.5,"V\u2080","m"],["l",177,60,232.5,60],["l",232.5,52.5,232.5,67.5],["l",243,60,235.5,60,"a"],["t",264,67.5,"U\u2091","m"],["l",279,60,294,60,"a"],["l",235.5,84,235.5,114,"a"],["t",249,135,"B\u2032","m"],["t",19.5,51,"B","m"],["t",22.5,108,"A","m"],["t",145.5,28.5,"f\u2080 = 40 cm","m"],["t",285,28.5,"f\u2091 = 4 cm","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments"},{id:"neet-2016-q162",pn:162,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"CE amplifier gains",q:"A npn transistor is connected to common emitter configuration in a given amplifier. A load resistance of 800 \u03A9 is connected in the collector circuit and the voltage drop across it is 0.8 V. If the current amplification factor is 0.96 and the input resistance of the circuit is 192 \u03A9, the voltage gain and the power gain of the amplifier will respectively be :",o:["3.69, 3.84","4, 4","4, 3.69","4, 3.84"],c:3,s:`Current gain \u03B2 = 0.96 (as given).
Voltage gain A_V = \u03B2 \xD7 R_L/R_i = 0.96 \xD7 800/192 = 4.
Power gain A_P = \u03B2 \xD7 A_V = 0.96 \xD7 4 = 3.84.
So voltage gain 4 and power gain 3.84.
Note: Printed key 4 is marked '#': the paper notes that the data are not physically consistent, since the current amplification factor of a common-emitter amplifier cannot be less than 1.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Semiconductor Electronics"},{id:"neet-2016-q163",pn:163,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Thermodynamics",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic work",q:"A gas is compressed isothermally to half its initial volume. The same gas is compressed separately through an adiabatic process until its volume is again reduced to half. Then :",o:["Compressing the gas through adiabatic process will require more work to be done.","Compressing the gas isothermally or adiabatically will require the same amount of work.","Which of the case (whether compression through isothermal or through adiabatic process) requires more work will depend upon the atomicity of the gas.","Compressing the gas isothermally will require more work to be done."],c:0,s:`Isothermal compression to V/2: P\u2082 = 2P\u2081.
Adiabatic compression to V/2: P\u2082 = 2^\u03B3 P\u2081, which is more than 2P\u2081 because \u03B3 > 1.
On a P-V graph the adiabatic curve lies above the isothermal one, so the area under it (work done) is larger.
So adiabatic compression needs more work.`,sfig:{w:350,h:238,alt:"P\u2013V graph: isothermal (I.T.) and adiabatic (A.D.) compression from (V, P\u2081) to V/2, ending at 2P\u2081 and 2\u1D5EP\u2081, arrows towards smaller volume.",d:[["l",58.8,200.2,58.8,28,"a"],["l",58.8,200.2,302.4,200.2,"a"],["l",58.8,158.2,240.8,158.2,"d"],["l",58.8,107.8,107.8,107.8,"d"],["l",58.8,71.4,268.8,71.4,"d"],["l",107.8,71.4,107.8,200.2,"d"],["l",240.8,71.4,240.8,200.2,"d"],["p","M107.8 71.4C133 114.8 175 148.4 240.8 158.2"],["p","M107.8 107.8C140 137.2 189 154 240.8 158.2"],["l",165.2,126,156.8,117.6,"a"],["l",163.8,142.8,152.6,135.8,"a"],["t",58.8,19.6,"P","m"],["t",313.6,205.8,"V","m"],["t",53.2,163.8,"P\u2081","e"],["t",53.2,113.4,"2P\u2081","e"],["t",53.2,77,"2\u1D5EP\u2081","e"],["t",107.8,221.2,"V/2","m"],["t",240.8,221.2,"V","m"],["t",137.2,86.8,"A.D."],["t",114.8,148.4,"I.T."]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Thermodynamics"},{id:"neet-2016-q164",pn:164,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Moving Charges and Magnetism",st:"Magnetic field of a thick wire",q:"A long straight wire of radius a carries a steady current I. The current uniformly distributed over its cross-section. The ratio of the magnetic fields B and B', at radial distance a/2 and 2a respectively, from the axis of the wire is :",o:["1/2","1","4","1/4"],c:1,s:`Inside (r = a/2): B = \u03BC\u2080I r/(2\u03C0a\xB2) = \u03BC\u2080I(a/2)/(2\u03C0a\xB2) = \u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0a).
Outside (r = 2a): B' = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0 \xD7 2a) = \u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0a).
B/B' = 1.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Moving Charges and Magnetism"},{id:"neet-2016-q165",pn:165,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments",st:"Mirror magnification",q:`Match the corresponding entries of column 1 with column 2. [Where m is the magnification produced by the mirror] :
Column 1: (A) m = -2 (B) m = -1/2 (C) m = +2 (D) m = +1/2
Column 2: (a) Convex mirror (b) Concave mirror (c) Real image (d) Virtual image`,o:["A \u2192 a and c; B \u2192 a and d; C \u2192 a and b; D \u2192 c and d","A \u2192 a and d; B \u2192 b and c; C \u2192 b and d; D \u2192 b and c","A \u2192 c and d; B \u2192 b and d; C \u2192 b and c; D \u2192 a and d","A \u2192 b and c; B \u2192 b and c; C \u2192 b and d; D \u2192 a and d"],c:3,s:`A negative m means an inverted, real image; a convex mirror never forms a real image, so the mirror is concave.
So m = -2 and m = -1/2: concave mirror, real image.
m = +2: erect, magnified, virtual image, which only a concave mirror (object inside the focus) gives.
m = +1/2: erect, diminished, virtual image, given only by a convex mirror.
So A \u2192 b, c; B \u2192 b, c; C \u2192 b, d; D \u2192 a, d.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments"},{id:"neet-2016-q166",pn:166,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Distance from a velocity equation",q:"If the velocity of a particle is v = At + Bt\xB2, where A and B are constants, then the distance travelled by it between 1 s and 2 s is :",o:["3A + 7B","3A/2 + 7B/3","A/2 + B/3","3A/2 + 4B"],c:1,s:`Distance = \u222Bv dt from t = 1 s to 2 s (v stays positive).
= [At\xB2/2 + Bt\xB3/3] from 1 to 2.
At t = 2: 2A + 8B/3. At t = 1: A/2 + B/3.
Difference = (2A - A/2) + (8B/3 - B/3) = 3A/2 + 7B/3.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Motion in a Straight Line"},{id:"neet-2016-q167",pn:167,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Rolling down an incline",q:"A disk and a sphere of same radius but different masses roll off on two inclined planes of the same altitude and length. Which one of the two objects gets to the bottom of the plane first ?",o:["Sphere","Both reach at the same time","Depends on their masses","Disk"],c:0,s:`For rolling without slipping: t = (1/sin\u03B8)\u221A((2L/g)(1 + k\xB2/R\xB2)).
For a disk k\xB2/R\xB2 = 1/2 = 0.5; for a solid sphere k\xB2/R\xB2 = 2/5 = 0.4.
The sphere has the smaller (1 + k\xB2/R\xB2), so it takes less time.
Mass does not matter, so the sphere reaches first.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion"},{id:"neet-2016-q168",pn:168,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Electric Charges and Fields",st:"Spheres with leaking charge",q:"Two identical charged spheres suspended from a common point by two massless strings of length l, are initially at a distance d (d << l) apart because of their mutual repulsion. The charges begin to leak from both the spheres at a constant rate. As a result, the spheres approach each other with a velocity v. Then v varies as a function of the distance x between the spheres, as :",o:["v \u221D x","v \u221D x^(-1/2)","v \u221D x^(-1)","v \u221D x^(1/2)"],c:1,s:`For each sphere: T sin\u03B8 = kq\xB2/d\xB2 and T cos\u03B8 = mg.
tan\u03B8 = kq\xB2/(mg d\xB2) = (d/2)/l (\u03B8 is small).
So d\xB3 = 2kq\xB2l/mg, so d\xB3 \u221D q\xB2.
For separation x: q \u221D x^(3/2).
Then dq/dt \u221D (3/2) x^(1/2) dx/dt = (3/2) x^(1/2) v.
dq/dt is constant, so x^(1/2) v = constant.
Hence v \u221D x^(-1/2).`,sfig:{w:260,h:185,alt:"Two charged spheres on strings of length \u2113 from one point, angle \u03B8 with the vertical, separation d; forces T, Tcos\u03B8, Tsin\u03B8, mg and kq\xB2/d\xB2 on the right sphere.",d:[["l",70,22,133,22],["l",100,22,100,140,"d"],["l",100,22,30,111],["l",100,22,171,109],["c",24,118,8],["c",179,117,8],["l",82,28,16,106,"b"],["t",50,66,"\u2113"],["p","M100 46A24 24 0 0 0 115 41"],["t",111,64,"\u03B8"],["l",168,104,148,78,"a"],["t",136,86,"T"],["p","M179 95A22 22 0 0 0 165 100"],["t",167,92,"\u03B8"],["l",179,109,179,68,"da"],["t",186,76,"Tcos\u03B8"],["l",171,117,133,117,"da"],["t",123,137,"Tsin\u03B8"],["l",187,117,228,117,"a"],["t",236,112,"kq\xB2"],["l",235,121,262,121],["t",242,134,"d\xB2"],["l",179,125,179,162,"a"],["t",179,176,"mg","m"],["l",95,155,27,155,"a"],["l",111,155,176,155,"a"],["t",103,159,"d","m"],["t",182,26,"\u03B8 = small"],["l",72,22,68,17],["l",76,22,72,17],["l",80,22,76,17],["l",84,22,80,17],["l",88,22,84,17],["l",92,22,88,17],["l",96,22,92,17],["l",100,22,96,17],["l",104,22,100,17],["l",108,22,104,17],["l",112,22,108,17],["l",116,22,112,17],["l",120,22,116,17],["l",124,22,120,17],["l",128,22,124,17],["l",132,22,128,17]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Electric Charges and Fields"},{id:"neet-2016-q169",pn:169,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Motion in a Plane",st:"Circular motion vectors",q:"A particle moves so that its position vector is given by r = cos \u03C9t x\u0302 + sin \u03C9t \u0177. Where \u03C9 is a constant. Which of the following is true ?",o:["Velocity and acceleration both are parallel to r","Velocity is perpendicular to r and acceleration is directed towards the origin.","Velocity is perpendicular to r and acceleration is directed away from the origin.","Velocity and acceleration both are perpendicular to r"],c:1,s:`v = dr/dt = -\u03C9 sin\u03C9t \xEE + \u03C9 cos\u03C9t \u0135.
r\xB7v = -\u03C9 sin\u03C9t cos\u03C9t + \u03C9 sin\u03C9t cos\u03C9t = 0, so v \u22A5 r.
a = dv/dt = -\u03C9\xB2(cos\u03C9t \xEE + sin\u03C9t \u0135) = -\u03C9\xB2 r.
The negative sign means the acceleration points towards the origin.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Motion in a Plane"},{id:"neet-2016-q170",pn:170,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Thermodynamics",st:"Heat from falling ice",q:"A piece of ice falls from a height h so that it melts completely. Only one-quarter of the heat produced is absorbed by the ice and all energy of ice gets converted into heat during its fall. The value of h is : [Latent heat of ice is 3.4 \xD7 10\u2075 J/kg and g = 10 N/kg]",o:["544 km","136 km","68 km","34 km"],c:1,s:`Heat produced = loss of potential energy = mgh.
One-quarter of it melts the ice: (1/4)mgh = mL.
h = 4L/g = (4 \xD7 3.4 \xD7 10\u2075)/10 = 1.36 \xD7 10\u2075 m = 136 km.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Thermodynamics"},{id:"neet-2016-q171",pn:171,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Net acceleration of a rotating disc",q:"A uniform circular disc of radius 50 cm at rest is free to turn about an axis which is perpendicular to its plane and passes through its centre. It is subjected to a torque which produces a constant angular acceleration of 2.0 rad s\u207B\xB2. Its net acceleration in m s\u207B\xB2 at the end of 2.0 s is approximately :",o:["7.0","6.0","3.0","8.0"],c:3,s:`\u03C9 = \u03C9\u2080 + \u03B1t = 0 + 2 \xD7 2 = 4 rad/s.
Centripetal acceleration a_R = \u03C9\xB2R = 16 \xD7 0.5 = 8 m/s\xB2.
Tangential acceleration a_t = \u03B1R = 2 \xD7 0.5 = 1 m/s\xB2.
Net a = \u221A(8\xB2 + 1\xB2) = \u221A65 \u2248 8.06, about 8 m/s\xB2.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion"},{id:"neet-2016-q172",pn:172,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Vertical loop",q:"What is the minimum velocity with which a body of mass m must enter a vertical loop of radius R so that it can complete the loop ?",o:["\u221A(2gR)","\u221A(3gR)","\u221A(5gR)","\u221A(gR)"],c:3,s:`At the top of the loop the minimum speed is \u221A(gR) (weight alone gives the centripetal force).
Energy conservation from the bottom to the top: \xBDmv\xB2 = \xBDm(gR) + mg(2R), so v = \u221A(5gR) at the bottom.
The printed key is \u221A(gR), which is the minimum speed at the top.
Note: Printed answer 4 (\u221AgR); my working gives (3) \u221A(5gR) for the speed on entering at the bottom, because \u221A(gR) is the minimum speed at the top of the loop. The paper's solution shows both values and keeps \u221AgR.`,sfig:{w:175,h:140,alt:"Vertical circle of radius R: block at the top with speed \u221AgR moving left, block at the bottom entering with speed \u221A5gR moving right.",d:[["c",80,70,54],["r",72,17,22,12],["r",70,110,23,12],["l",72,22,41,22,"a"],["t",36,28,"\u221AgR","e"],["l",93,116,121,121,"a"],["t",125,126,"\u221A5gR"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Work, Energy and Power"},{id:"neet-2016-q173",pn:173,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Capacitor in AC",q:"A small signal voltage V(t) = V\u2080 sin \u03C9t is applied across an ideal capacitor C :",o:["Over a full cycle the capacitor C does not consume any energy from the voltage source","Current I(t) is in phase with voltage V(t)","Current I(t) leads voltage V(t) by 180\xBA","Current I(t), lags voltage V(t) by 90\xBA"],c:0,s:`In an ideal capacitor the current leads the voltage by 90\xBA, so (2), (3) and (4) are wrong.
The average power over a full cycle is V_rms I_rms cos90\xBA = 0.
So the capacitor consumes no energy over a cycle.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Alternating Current"},{id:"neet-2016-q174",pn:174,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Waves",st:"Wave on a hanging rope",q:"A uniform rope of length L and mass m\u2081 hangs vertically from a rigid support. A block of mass m\u2082 is attached to the free end of the rope. A transverse pulse of wavelength \u03BB\u2081 is produced at the lower end of the rope. The wavelength of the pulse when it reaches the top of the rope is \u03BB\u2082. The ratio \u03BB\u2082/\u03BB\u2081 is :",o:["\u221A((m\u2081 + m\u2082)/m\u2082)","\u221A(m\u2082/m\u2081)","\u221A((m\u2081 + m\u2082)/m\u2081)","\u221A(m\u2081/m\u2082)"],c:0,s:`Wave speed v = f\u03BB = \u221A(T/\u03BC), and the frequency f stays the same, so \u03BB \u221D \u221AT.
Tension at the bottom: T\u2081 = m\u2082g.
Tension at the top: T\u2082 = (m\u2081 + m\u2082)g.
\u03BB\u2082/\u03BB\u2081 = \u221A(T\u2082/T\u2081) = \u221A((m\u2081 + m\u2082)/m\u2082).`,sfig:{w:100,h:140,alt:"Rope of mass m\u2081 hanging from a rigid support with a block of mass m\u2082 at its lower end.",d:[["l",5,11,63,11],["l",9,11,5,6],["l",14,11,10,6],["l",19,11,15,6],["l",24,11,20,6],["l",29,11,25,6],["l",34,11,30,6],["l",39,11,35,6],["l",44,11,40,6],["l",49,11,45,6],["l",54,11,50,6],["l",59,11,55,6],["l",32,12,32,100],["l",38,12,38,100],["r",16,100,36,25,"f"],["t",44,60,"m\u2081"],["t",58,118,"m\u2082"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Waves"},{id:"neet-2016-q175",pn:175,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Power in series LCR",q:"An inductor 20 mH, a capacitor 50 \u03BCF and a resistor 40 \u03A9 are connected in series across a source of emf V = 10 sin 340t. The power loss in A.C. circuit is :",o:["0.67 W","0.76 W","0.89 W","0.51 W"],c:3,s:`\u03C9 = 340 rad/s.
X_L = \u03C9L = 340 \xD7 20 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 6.8 \u03A9.
X_C = 1/(\u03C9C) = 1/(340 \xD7 50 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 58.8 \u03A9.
Net reactance = 58.8 - 6.8 = 52 \u03A9.
Z = \u221A(40\xB2 + 52\xB2) = \u221A4304 = 65.6 \u03A9.
P = V_rms I_rms cos\u03C6 = (10/\u221A2)(10/(\u221A2 \xD7 65.6))(40/65.6) = 0.46 W.
The nearest option is 0.51 W.
Note: printed answer 4 (0.51 W); my working gives 0.46 W (as the paper's own working also does), and 0.51 W is the nearest given option.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Alternating Current"},{id:"neet-2016-q176",pn:176,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength",q:"An electron of mass m and a photon have same energy E. The ratio of de-Broglie wavelengths associated with them is : (c being velocity of light)",o:["(E/2m)^(1/2)","c(2mE)^(1/2)","(1/c)(2m/E)^(1/2)","(1/c)(E/2m)^(1/2)"],c:3,s:`Electron: p = \u221A(2mE), so \u03BB_e = h/\u221A(2mE).
Photon: E = hc/\u03BB, so \u03BB_ph = hc/E.
\u03BB_e/\u03BB_ph = [h/\u221A(2mE)] \xD7 [E/(hc)] = E/(c\u221A(2mE)) = (1/c)\u221A(E/2m).`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter"},{id:"neet-2016-q177",pn:177,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Atoms",st:"Distance of closest approach",q:"When an \u03B1-particle of mass 'm' moving with velocity 'v' bombards on a heavy nucleus of charge 'Ze', its distance of closest approach from the nucleus depends on m as :",o:["1/\u221Am","1/m\xB2","m","1/m"],c:3,s:`At the closest approach the whole kinetic energy has become electrostatic potential energy.
\xBDmv\xB2 = k(Ze)(2e)/r.
r = 4kZe\xB2/(mv\xB2).
So r \u221D 1/m for a given v.`,sfig:{w:375,h:110,alt:"Heavy nucleus +Ze at left; \u03B1-particle (+2e) arriving from \u221E with velocity v, stopping at distance r.",d:[["c",31,45,27],["t",31,50,"+Ze","m"],["t",137,38,"v = ?","m"],["t",136,66,"*","m"],["l",78,90,28,90,"a"],["t",86,94,"r","m"],["l",94,90,140,90,"a"],["l",267,102,267,26,"da"],["t",267,16,"\u221E","m"],["l",307,63,227,63,"a"],["t",222,50,"v","m"],["t",315,70,"\u03B1(+2e)"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Atoms"},{id:"neet-2016-q178",pn:178,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Thermodynamics",st:"Refrigerator power",q:"A refrigerator works between 4\xBAC and 30\xBAC. It is required to remove 600 calories of heat every second in order to keep the temperature of the refrigerated space constant. The power required is : (Take 1 cal = 4.2 Joules)",o:["23.65 W","236.5 W","2365 W","2.365 W"],c:1,s:`T\u2081 = 4 + 273 = 277 K (cold), T\u2082 = 30 + 273 = 303 K (hot).
COP = Q\u2081/W = T\u2081/(T\u2082 - T\u2081).
Heat removed per second Q\u2081 = 600 \xD7 4.2 = 2520 J.
W = Q\u2081(T\u2082 - T\u2081)/T\u2081 = 2520 \xD7 26/277 = 236.5 J per second.
Power = 236.5 W.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Thermodynamics"},{id:"neet-2016-q179",pn:179,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Motion in a Plane",st:"Tangential acceleration",q:"A particle of mass 10 g moves along a circle of radius 6.4 cm with a constant tangential acceleration. What is the magnitude of this acceleration if the kinetic energy of the particle becomes equal to 8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J by the end of the second revolution after the beginning of the motion ?",o:["0.15 m/s\xB2","0.18 m/s\xB2","0.2 m/s\xB2","0.1 m/s\xB2"],c:3,s:`KE = \xBDmv\xB2 = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J with m = 10 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 kg.
v\xB2 = 16 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 = 0.16, so v = 0.4 m/s.
Distance in two revolutions: s = 2 \xD7 2\u03C0r = 4\u03C0 \xD7 0.064 = 0.804 m.
v\xB2 = u\xB2 + 2as with u = 0: 0.16 = 2a(0.804).
a = 0.16/1.608 \u2248 0.1 m/s\xB2.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Motion in a Plane"},{id:"neet-2016-q180",pn:180,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments",st:"Prism at minimum deviation",q:"The angle of incidence for a ray of light at a refracting surface of a prism is 45\xBA. The angle of prism is 60\xBA. If the ray suffers minimum deviation through the prism, the angle of minimum deviation and refractive index of the material of the prism respectively, are :",o:["30\xBA ; \u221A2","45\xBA ; \u221A2","30\xBA ; 1/\u221A2","45\xBA ; 1/\u221A2"],c:0,s:`At minimum deviation i = (\u03B4_m + A)/2.
45\xBA = (\u03B4_m + 60\xBA)/2, so \u03B4_m = 30\xBA.
Also r = A/2 = 30\xBA.
Snell's law: sin45\xBA = \u03BC sin30\xBA, so 1/\u221A2 = \u03BC/2.
\u03BC = \u221A2.`,sfig:{w:200,h:160,alt:"Prism of apex angle A = 60\xB0 with a ray entering the left face at i = 45\xB0, travelling parallel to the base inside with r = A/2 = 30\xB0.",d:[["p","M112 28L43 147H180Z"],["l",4,98.4,84,77],["l",30,91.5,40,88.8,"a"],["l",84,77,140,77],["l",108,77,120,77,"a"],["l",58,62,84,77,"d"],["l",84,77,108,91,"d"],["p","M66.7 67A20 20 0 0 0 64.7 82.2"],["p","M104 77A20 20 0 0 1 101.3 87"],["t",46,68,"i = 45\xB0","e"],["t",118,24,"A = 60\xB0"],["t",104,104,"r = A/2"],["t",104,118,"= 30\xB0"],["t",112,140,"\u03BC","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments"}]})();
